var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/Config/#Config","page":"Config","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Config"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config","page":"Config","text":"Config\n\nThe loader of a consumer's .gbg/config.toml. Config.read is the one way the config is obtained — by every verb and by the makedocs wrapper — and Config.ConfigError is what a config that cannot be used raises; Config.find_root finds the consumer root a directory lies in, and Config.check_paths runs every existence check the config implies (the doctor's CFG.SURFACES). The helpers Config.glob_files, over Config.glob_to_regex, and Config.resolve_anchor are the two readers of the config's path vocabulary (globs and file#heading anchors), shared with the doctor and the scans.\n\nTwo words this package overloads, both first met here. A SURFACE is the config's unit of \"what gets read\": a named set of files — rationale, pages, src, ext, literate, data, and the compounds docs and all — that every scan, engine, doctor check and instrument works over. An ANCHOR here is a file#heading reference into a document, and NOTHING else: elsewhere the same word is a hat or lane that runs at full strength rather than on the economy model (Preflight.resolve_hats), and a regular expression marking where a seed is planted (Seeds.select). Three unrelated senses, one word.\n\nThe ECONOMY MODEL is the cheaper AI model the tool's model catalogue names (Preflight.economy_model): in an economy round every lane and hat that is not an anchor runs on it, and an anchor runs at full strength, on the session's own model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.ConfigError","page":"Config","text":"ConfigError <: Exception\n\nA config that cannot be used: absent, unparsable, invalid against the schema, or naming a path that does not resolve. The fields are:\n\nmsg — the message, with every violation listed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.ConfigError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.ConfigWrittenError","page":"Config","text":"ConfigWrittenError <: Exception\n\nA config the running verb wrote ITSELF did not read back. The fields are:\n\nmsg — the underlying ConfigError's message.\n\nDeliberately not a subtype of ConfigError. The command line reports a ConfigError as exit 2, \"cannot evaluate\": the consumer's config is bad and the consumer can fix it. A config this tool has just written failing to validate is a defect of the tool, not of the consumer, so it must reach the crash path — exit 3 with its backtrace — and a subtype would be caught as the consumer's mistake.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.ConfigWrittenError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.find_root","page":"Config","text":"find_root(start = pwd()) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nWalks up from start to the first directory holding .gbg/config.toml — the consumer root, the same walk the shim does. Returns that directory, or nothing when none is found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.find_root"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.glob_files","page":"Config","text":"glob_files(root, pattern) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery regular file under root whose /-separated relative path matches the glob pattern (see glob_to_regex), as relative paths, sorted. A pattern without a wildcard names one file and matches it if it exists. Returns the list, empty when nothing matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.glob_files"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.glob_to_regex","page":"Config","text":"glob_to_regex(pattern) -> Regex\n\nTurns a glob over /-separated relative paths into a regex: ** matches any run of directories (including none), * any run of characters within one segment, ? one character. Returns the anchored regex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.glob_to_regex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.check_paths","page":"Config","text":"check_paths(cfg) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery existence check the config implies (the doctor's CFG.SURFACES): each non-optional surface glob matches at least one file; each file#heading anchor resolves to exactly one heading; each module has a module <Name> block under src/; each [paths] entry is a directory; the glossary file exists and a non-empty built_html has a docs/ directory beside it; each extensions file, the docs/make.jl the config schema pins docs_build to name, each executable_claims file and, while seed_rounds is on, the seed catalogue exist. Returns the problems found, empty when all hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.check_paths"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/doctor/#The-doctor's-checks","page":"The doctor's checks","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nGenerated at build time from the doctor's own table (Doctor.CHECKS): every stable id, its family, whether the QUICK set runs it, whether it needs --deep, and whether a scope = \"check\" ledger entry may exempt it. The check's own detail line says what failed and what fixes it; ARCHITECTURE §7.3 is the authoritative table with the failure conditions.\n\nId Family QUICK --deep Exemptable\nENV.JULIA ENV   no\nENV.SHIM ENV   no\nENV.DEV_CHECKOUT ENV   no\nSHIM.HASH SHIM   no\nPIN.CONFIG PIN yes  no\nPIN.SOURCES PIN yes  no\nPIN.TREESHA PIN   no\nPIN.SELF PIN   no\nPIN.NOT_DEV PIN yes  no\nSELF.CONSUMER SELF yes  no\nCFG.VALID CFG yes  no\nCFG.TORN CFG   no\nCFG.SURFACES CFG yes  no\nCFG.HATS CFG   no\nCFG.SEEDS CFG   no\nCFG.TERRITORIES CFG yes  no\nCFG.EXEMPTIONS CFG yes  no\nEXEMPT.EXPIRED EXEMPT yes  no\nEXEMPT.EXPIRING EXEMPT yes  no\nEXEMPT.STALE EXEMPT yes  no\nEXEMPT.BROAD EXEMPT yes  no\nWIRE.MAKEDOCS WIRE yes  no\nWIRE.RUNTESTS WIRE yes  no\nWIRE.CI_AUTH WIRE yes  no\nWIRE.GITIGNORE WIRE yes  no\nWIRE.CLAUDE_MD WIRE yes  no\nMANAGED.HASH MANAGED yes  no\nMANAGED.STALE MANAGED   no\nLEG.COMMANDS LEG   yes\nLEG.PILES LEG   yes\nLEG.LINTS LEG   yes\nLEG.SENTINELS LEG   yes\nPOL.IMPORT POL   no\nPOL.SENTINEL POL yes  no\nPOL.COLLAPSED POL   no\nGIT.BRANCH GIT   yes\nGIT.TRAILER GIT   yes\nLOAD.ENVS LOAD  yes no\nBUILD.DOCS BUILD  yes no\nSELF.DOCS SELF  yes no\nTEST.PKG TEST  yes no","title":"The doctor's checks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/install/#install","page":"Install","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nThe tool reaches a machine in two ways, both per machine and never synced: the pinned copy Pkg fetches into the consumer's docs/ environment, and the shim ~/.local/bin/gbg that executes it. A consumer is a package that pins this tool and is reviewed by it — one of the four GBG science packages, the tool itself, which is its own fifth consumer, or the toy fixture under fixture/ the tests run over; the glossary binds the term.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/install/#The-pinned-version","page":"Install","text":"Every sub-environment of a consumer that lists the tool (docs/, test/, benchmark/) carries the same three entries, rendered by gbg sync from the pinned version in .gbg/config.toml:\n\n[deps]\nGBGCodingTools = \"3aee1de0-bb62-4679-b1c5-a861fbcaaa51\"\n[sources]\nGBGCodingTools = {url = \"https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGCodingTools.jl.git\", rev = \"v1.0.0\"}\n[compat]\nGBGCodingTools = \"=1.0.0\"\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()' fetches the tagged tree. The tool is never a dependency of the package proper.","title":"The pinned version"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/install/#The-shim","page":"Install","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=docs -e 'using GBGCodingTools; exit(GBGCodingTools.main(ARGS))' -- install --dev-checkout \"<dev checkout>\"\n\ngbg install copies bin/gbg to ~/.local/bin/gbg and, with the option, writes ~/.gbg/dev. The shim is POSIX sh: it walks up to the nearest .gbg/config.toml, reads the pin only to tell dev from a release, and executes julia --project=<root>/docs on the copy that environment resolved — so gbg in a consumer runs the version its docs/ environment resolved, which is the pinned one once that environment is instantiated after a bump. One consumer is routed differently: a consumer pinned at dev that is not itself the tool's checkout — the fixture, which sits inside that checkout — resolves against the enclosing tool repository's root rather than its own docs/. gbg --dev … runs the registered dev checkout instead; GBG_DEV=1 marks it.","title":"The shim"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/install/#The-dev-checkout","page":"Install","text":"The owner's working tree of the tool is a consumer of itself: gbg --dev init at its root writes a config pinned dev, gbg --dev sync renders its managed files, and its docs/Project.toml resolves the tool by path = \"..\". The doctor's SELF.CONSUMER check holds this in place.","title":"The dev checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ledger/#ledger","page":"The ledger","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\n.gbg/exemptions.toml is the one place a consumer — a package that pins this tool and is reviewed by it — records a waiver or an adjudication. One [[exemption]] per entry:\n\n[[exemption]]\nid       = \"RULE.DOC.STEER\"       # a RULE.* rule, a SCAN.* scan, a doctor check, a finding id, or a harness op\nscope    = \"policy\"               # policy | scan | lint | check | finding | harness\ntarget   = \"*\"                    # or a symbol, a file, a glob, a finding id\nreason   = \"at least 40 characters: what is declined and why\"\nrecorded = 2026-08-25\nexpires  = 2027-08-25             # required for every scope but finding; at most recorded + 365 days\nevidence = \"IT CLAUDE.md:179-182\"\n\nA scope = \"finding\" entry is the adjudication channel: it carries overturn_when instead of expires, closes exactly one banked finding, and is audited by the dismissal auditor at close. Its optional ruling says which kind of adjudication it is: not-a-defect (the default) rules the finding no defect, and its reason reaches every later panel and verifier as settled; scoped puts a real defect outside the round's reach, closes it, and is never shown as settled. An optional [exemption.match] table (arg_name, arg_type, path, symbol, ANDed) refines a scan, lint, policy or harness entry: the engine applies the predicate per trigger and the rule stays live elsewhere; arg_type is evaluable by the reflection scans only, and a text scan refuses it.\n\nThe doctor's CFG.EXEMPTIONS validates every entry; EXEMPT.EXPIRED fails on a lapsed one, EXEMPT.EXPIRING warns within 30 days, EXEMPT.STALE on a target that no longer exists, and EXEMPT.BROAD when one match covers more than half of its rule's triggers. The families PIN.*, CFG.*, EXEMPT.*, WIRE.*, MANAGED.*, SHIM.*, POL.*, ROUND.*, SELF.* are never exemptable.","title":"The ledger"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/ledger/#GBGCodingTools.Ledger.read","page":"The ledger","text":"Ledger.read(cfg) -> Vector{Dict{String,Any}}\n\nEvery [[exemption]] of <root>/.gbg/exemptions.toml, schema-validated. Returns an empty vector when the file is absent (an empty ledger is a legal state) and throws a LedgerError when it does not parse or does not validate — an unreadable ledger is never treated as empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Ledger.read"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/ledger/#GBGCodingTools.Ledger.entries","page":"The ledger","text":"Ledger.entries(ledger, id; scope = nothing, live = true) -> Vector\n\nThe entries naming an id. The arguments are:\n\nledger — the entries Ledger.read returned.\nid — the id to select.\nscope — one scope to restrict to (any when nothing).\nlive — whether to drop an entry past its expires date (it no longer exempts anything, and the doctor reports it).\n\nReturns the selection in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Ledger.entries"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/ledger/#GBGCodingTools.Ledger.matches","page":"The ledger","text":"Ledger.matches(entry, root; file = \"\", symbol = \"\",\n               arg_name = nothing, arg_type = nothing) -> Bool\n\nWhether entry covers a trigger. The arguments are:\n\nentry — the ledger entry.\nroot — the consumer root; globs in target and match.path resolve against the file's relative path, so root only names the tree.\nfile — the trigger's file, relative to the root.\nsymbol — the trigger's owning binding.\narg_name — the argument name, for a reflection scan's trigger.\narg_type — the argument's declared type, for a reflection scan's trigger; a text scan passes nothing and refuses an entry carrying match.arg_type before calling — this function throws on it.\n\nThe entry covers the trigger when its target is *, equals file or symbol, or is a glob matching file; and, when it carries a match table, every present sub-key holds (path a glob over file, symbol a regex over symbol, arg_name a regex, arg_type a type name tested by <:). Returns the boolean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Ledger.matches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/CLI/#CLI-internals","page":"CLI internals","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nThe engines behind the verbs, documented at their homes.","title":"CLI internals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/CLI/#The-wrapper-and-the-dispatcher","page":"CLI internals","text":"","title":"The wrapper and the dispatcher"},{"category":"section","location":"api/CLI/#Engines","page":"CLI internals","text":"","title":"Engines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.makedocs","page":"CLI internals","text":"makedocs(cfg; kwargs...)\n\nThe mandatory docs-build wrapper a consumer's docs/make.jl calls instead of Documenter.makedocs. The body is defined by the Documenter extension (ext/GBGCodingToolsDocumenterExt.jl), which Julia loads once Documenter is in the environment — a consumer's make.jl already does using Documenter.\n\nThe arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config object Config.read returns; it names the surfaces the scans read.\nkwargs... — passed through to Documenter.makedocs unchanged (sitename, pages, modules, checkdocs, doctest, format, …).\n\nBefore Documenter runs it runs, in-process, the quick doctor set — the :QUICK selection of GBGCodingTools.doctor, the checks that hold on any machine with no build and no network — and the hard scans over the source surfaces; after Documenter it runs the built-HTML scans. Any non-zero return is an error, so the build fails. It prints one banner line, GBGCodingTools v<ver> makedocs, which a consumer's CI greps for.\n\nA build reads no review state and nothing can freeze it: the closure gate that used to refuse a build while a round was open went with the round machinery on 2026-09-17, because a check that runs inside a consumer's build must give the same answer on a fresh clone and on CI.\n\nReturns what Documenter.makedocs returns, true when that is nothing — as it is unless debug = true is passed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.makedocs"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.VERBS","page":"CLI internals","text":"VERBS\n\nThe closed verb table of the command line: \"<noun> <verb>\" (or a lone \"<noun>\") to the function that runs it. Every entry takes the remaining arguments as a Vector{String} and returns the exit code as an Int. Each src/CLI/*.jl file registers its own verbs at load; nothing else adds to the table, and a verb that is not in it does not exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.VERBS"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync","page":"CLI internals","text":"Sync\n\nRenders and checks the managed footprint of a consumer — a managed file is one the tool renders into the consumer and hash-checks thereafter, edited only by gbg sync, never by hand: the skill stubs, the Stop hook and its settings.json, the policy copy under .gbg/policy/, the regressions README, the managed blocks of CLAUDE.md and .gitignore, the tool's entries in every sub-environment Project.toml, the CI fragments, and the sidecar .gbg/managed.json. Sync.plan computes what every managed file must contain; Sync.status compares the tree with it; Sync.sync writes. Two of the parts it renders are documented on their own: Sync.managed_block, the managed block of CLAUDE.md or .gitignore, and Sync.subenv_entries, the tool's [deps], [sources] and [compat] entries in one sub-environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.plan","page":"CLI internals","text":"plan(cfg; ledger = Ledger.read(cfg)) -> Vector{ManagedFile}\n\nEvery generated-managed file for a consumer: one skill stub per enabled instrument (GBGCodingTools binds the term) plus gbg-doctor, .claude/settings.json, .claude/hooks/queue-guard.sh, the policy copy and test/regressions/README.md. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nledger — the ledger entries.\n\nThe two managed blocks and the sub-environment entries are planned by managed_block and subenv_entries. Returns the list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.status","page":"CLI internals","text":"status(cfg; ledger = Ledger.read(cfg)) -> Vector{Entry}\n\nCompares the tree with a fresh render of every managed file and block, and lists sidecar entries whose file is no longer planned (ORPHAN). The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nledger — the ledger entries.\n\nReturns the entries; MANAGED.HASH fails on any MODIFIED or UNMANAGED, MANAGED.STALE on any STALE, MISSING or ORPHAN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.status"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.render","page":"CLI internals","text":"render(text, slots) -> String\n\nSubstitutes every {{key}} in text from slots. A key with no value is a SyncError, never an empty string. Returns the rendered text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.render"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.managed_block","page":"CLI internals","text":"managed_block(cfg, file, s) -> String\n\nThe rendered managed block for one file. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nfile — \"CLAUDE.md\".\ns — the slot values.\n\nThe header's sha256 is that of the body lines between the markers, so an edited body no longer matches its header. Returns the block text ending in a newline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.managed_block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.subenv_entries","page":"CLI internals","text":"subenv_entries(cfg, envdir) -> Dict\n\nThe [deps], [sources] and [compat] entries for the tool in the sub-environment at envdir (relative to the root): pinned at a release, the https url and rev = \"v<version>\" with [compat] = \"=<version>\"; pinned at dev, a path source resolving to the tool repository and no compat. Returns Dict(\"deps\" => uuid, \"sources\" => Dict, \"compat\" => String or nothing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.subenv_entries"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Init","page":"CLI internals","text":"Init\n\nSeeds a consumer: Init.derive_config reads the package tree into a config that validates, Init.init writes .gbg/ and the seeded-once files and renders the tool's entries into every sub-environment, Init.migrate_baselines converts the three Julia test literals to their TOML form textually, Init.migrate_seeds converts a former seed_defects.py catalogue, and Init.new adds a fifth. The verb renders no generated file (the skill stubs, the hook, the CI fragments, the managed blocks): that is gbg sync, which validates what init wrote.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Init.derive_config","page":"CLI internals","text":"derive_config(root) -> Dict\n\nThe best config the package tree supports: name and description from Project.toml, modules from the module declarations under src/, the surfaces that exist, the history and references anchors found by heading, the executable claims and guard symbols present, one bug-hunt territory per source file, the eight generic hats. The tool root gets its own delta, and a tree inside the tool repository is pinned dev. The result validates against the schema with defaults materialised; a value the tree cannot supply is written with a TODO the doctor will flag, never invented. Returns the config (without root).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init.derive_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Init.migrate_seeds","page":"CLI internals","text":"migrate_seeds(root, cfg; io = stdout) -> Int\n\nConverts a former .claude/commands/check-consistency/seed_defects.py CATALOG (tuples of class, description, anchor regex, replacement, occurrence) into .gbg/seeds.toml as the seeds definition states. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the package root.\ncfg — the derived config (for the package name).\nio — where the report goes.\n\nAn absent source writes nothing, exit 0; an existing seeds.toml is left alone. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init.migrate_seeds"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Ledger","page":"CLI internals","text":"Ledger\n\nThe reader of a consumer's exemption ledger .gbg/exemptions.toml: Ledger.read validates the file against schema/exemptions.schema.json and returns its entries, Ledger.entries selects the ones naming an id, and Ledger.matches applies an entry's optional match table to a trigger. The doctor's CFG.EXEMPTIONS check applies the ledger's semantic rules (expiry bounds, the non-exemptable prefixes); the reader only parses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Ledger"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Doctor","page":"CLI internals","text":"Doctor\n\nThe health check of a consumer's tooling: every check reads the tree and writes nothing, except the --deep set, which runs the package's builds and tests. Every check has a stable id, returns PASS, WARN, FAIL or NA, and can be selected by id or prefix; the QUICK set is the subset that holds on a runner with no shim and a detached HEAD. A scope = \"check\" ledger entry on an exemptable id (LEG.*, GIT.*, AUDIT.*) turns its FAIL into a WARN naming the reason; the non-exemptable families are the tool's own enforcement.\n\nThe word anchor carries three senses here, and the checks below touch two: a hat's or lane's anchor is whether it runs at full strength rather than on the economy model that GBGCodingTools.Config defines (CFG.HATS); Config.resolve_anchor's is a file#heading Markdown reference; a seed class's, in Seeds.select, is the regular expression marking where the seed is planted. No one of them implies another.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Doctor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Doctor.select","page":"CLI internals","text":"select(only; deep = false) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe check ids a run covers. The arguments are:\n\nonly — the ids the run is restricted to: exact ids, prefixes such as LOAD or WIRE, or the literal QUICK; every check when nothing.\ndeep — include the deep checks in the default set (it does not filter the ids only names).\n\nReturns the ids in table order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Doctor.select"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git\n\nThin readers over a consumer's repository: Git.run returns a command's output or nothing, Git.head, Git.is_ancestor, Git.porcelain (modified and untracked paths), Git.diff, Git.log. Nothing here writes; git add is never called by the tool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.run","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.run(root, args...) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\ngit -C root args... with stderr discarded; the stdout with TRAILING whitespace removed, or nothing on a non-zero exit or when git is absent.\n\nTrailing only, never leading. git status --porcelain puts the status in the first two columns, so an unstaged modification begins with a SPACE (\" M path\"), and a full strip deleted it — from the first line alone, since only that line touches the start of the output. Git.porcelain then read the code as \"M \" and the path from column 4 as gbg/exemptions.toml, one character short, matching no watched path.\n\nSo CFG.TORN called a modified pinned file unmodified whenever that file sorted first, and sort order is what hid it: with any other file ahead of it the pinned one kept its space and the guard fired normally (2026-09-12-check-docs-01, work queue). --stat output is indented the same way and was losing its first line's alignment for the same reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.run"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.head","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.head(root) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nThe full sha of HEAD, or nothing outside a repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.head"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.is_ancestor","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.is_ancestor(root, a, b) -> Bool\n\nWhether one commit is an ancestor of (or equal to) another (git merge-base --is-ancestor). The arguments are:\n\nroot — the repository the two commits are read in.\na — the commit tested as the ancestor.\nb — the commit tested as the descendant; a equal to b counts as an ancestor.\n\nReturns the boolean; false outside a repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.is_ancestor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.porcelain","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.porcelain(root; untracked = true) -> Vector{Tuple{String,String}}\n\ngit status --porcelain seen from a consumer, as (status, path) pairs with paths relative to the consumer root. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the consumer root the status is read from and the paths are made relative to.\nuntracked — with untracked = false untracked files are left out.\n\nPorcelain paths are relative to the repository top, so for a consumer nested inside a larger repository (the fixture inside the tool's) they are translated and paths outside the consumer are dropped — the tool's own modified files are not the fixture's. Empty outside a repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.porcelain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.diff","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.diff(root, range, paths) -> String\n\nThe diff of a commit range restricted to a set of paths. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the repository the diff is taken in.\nrange — the commit range, <base>..<head>.\npaths — the paths the diff is restricted to.\n\nReturns the patch text, empty outside a repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.diff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Git.log","page":"CLI internals","text":"Git.log(root, range) -> String\n\ngit log --format=\"%h %s\" range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Git.log"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker","page":"CLI internals","text":"HtmlWalker\n\nThe one HTML reader of the tool: Gumbo parses a built page (HtmlWalker.parse_page), and these helpers walk it — the article element Documenter renders into (HtmlWalker.article_of), the text of a node (HtmlWalker.text_of), the elements matching a predicate (HtmlWalker.elements), and a node's tag, classes and attributes (HtmlWalker.tagname, HtmlWalker.classes, HtmlWalker.attr). Both the consolidator and the built-HTML scans go through here, so the rendered site is read one way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.parse_page","page":"CLI internals","text":"parse_page(path) -> HTMLDocument\n\nParses the HTML file at path with Gumbo, keeping whitespace-only text nodes (Gumbo.jl drops them by default, and a dropped space between two inline elements changes the reader text — the 1.6 byte comparison against the Python consolidator is what caught it). Returns the document; its root is doc.root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.parse_page"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.tagname","page":"CLI internals","text":"tagname(node) -> Symbol\n\nThe tag of an element node (:div, :p, …), or :text for a text node (Gumbo drops comments at parse time, so no comment node exists). Returns the symbol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.tagname"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.classes","page":"CLI internals","text":"classes(el) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe class attribute of el split on whitespace, empty for a node without one. Returns the list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.classes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.attr","page":"CLI internals","text":"attr(el, name, default = \"\") -> String\n\nThe named attribute of a node, or a fallback. The arguments are:\n\nel — the node; anything that is not an HTMLElement (a text node) carries no attributes and always takes default.\nname — the attribute name.\ndefault — the value taken when el has no such attribute; \"\" unless given.\n\nReturns the string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.attr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.elements","page":"CLI internals","text":"elements(root, pred) -> Vector\n\nEvery element node under root (inclusive, document order) for which pred(el) is true. Returns the list; pred receives HTMLElements only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.elements"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.text_of","page":"CLI internals","text":"text_of(node) -> String\n\nThe concatenated text of every text node under node (comments excluded), as BeautifulSoup's get_text() would give it. Returns the string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.text_of"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.article_of","page":"CLI internals","text":"article_of(doc) -> Union{HTMLElement,Nothing}\n\nThe <article class=\"content\"> Documenter renders a page's body into, or nothing when the page has none (the search page, an asset). Returns the element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.HtmlWalker.article_of"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.consolidate","page":"CLI internals","text":"consolidate(build_dir, withheld; out_full, out_siteonly) -> (pages, nfull, nsite)\n\nRenders every page of the Documenter build under build_dir into reader text — headings as #, code as fences, tables as | rows, admonition titles as [ADMONITION], list items as -, formulas as their raw TeX (Documenter renders KaTeX client-side, so the TeX is the page's text) — delimited by ===== PAGE: <name> ===== banners, discovered from the build so the manuscript stays complete when pages are added. Writes two manuscripts. The arguments are:\n\nbuild_dir — the Documenter build whose pages are discovered and rendered.\nwithheld — the words that withhold a page (the design documents the config names under design_docs_withheld).\nout_full — the manuscript written with every page.\nout_siteonly — the manuscript written without the pages whose relative path contains one of the withheld words.\n\nReturns the page count and the two character counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.consolidate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.Hit","page":"CLI internals","text":"Hit(file, line, text; symbol = \"\")\n\nOne scan hit. The fields are:\n\nfile — the surface file, relative to the consumer root.\nline — the 1-based line, or 0 when the hit is about the whole file.\ntext — the excerpt or message a reader needs.\nsymbol — the owning binding when the scan knows it (what a ledger entry's symbol predicate is matched against); empty otherwise.\n\nThe arguments of the constructor are the fields, symbol as a keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Hit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.ScanResult","page":"CLI internals","text":"ScanResult\n\nThe outcome of one scan. The fields are:\n\nid — the scan id.\nrule — the rule it enforces.\ntier — hard or triage, the argcov_policy tier resolved from config.\nhits — the surviving hits.\npopulation — what the scan measured (files, docstrings, exports, arguments or pages); zero means the scan measured nothing and is DEAD, whatever the ledger removed.\nexempted — the number of hits the ledger removed.\nnote — a scan-level message: an error, a reason a lane is dead (a lane being one agent's share of a round, bound in GBGCodingTools), empty otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.ScanResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.register_scan!","page":"CLI internals","text":"register_scan!(id, f)\n\nRegisters f as the scan id. The contract: f(cfg, files) receives the validated config and the surface files (relative paths) the catalogue resolves for the scan, and returns (hits::Vector{Hit}, population::Int), or (hits, population, note::AbstractString) when the scan owes the reader something about the measurement itself, which a DEAD line then carries. An HTML scan receives the built pages instead of source files. Returns f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.register_scan!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.prose_lines","page":"CLI internals","text":"prose_lines(cfg, file) -> Vector{ProseLine}\n\nThe rendered-prose lines of one surface file: a Markdown file outside its fenced blocks; a Julia file only inside \"\"\" docstrings (a one-line \"\"\"…\"\"\" docstring is one prose line); a Literate file's full-line # comments with the marker stripped, outside fences. Fence lines themselves are not returned. Returns the lines; an empty vector for a file with no prose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.prose_lines"},{"category":"module","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.TreeHash","page":"CLI internals","text":"TreeHash\n\nGit's content hashing, reimplemented on the SHA stdlib so the running copy can re-hash itself without git: TreeHash.blob_hash and TreeHash.tree_hash reproduce the object ids git hash-object and git write-tree would give, which is what Pkg records as git-tree-sha1 in a manifest and reports as tree_hash from Pkg.dependencies(). gbg self verify compares the two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TreeHash"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.TreeHash.tree_hash","page":"CLI internals","text":"tree_hash(dir; skip = (\".git\",)) -> String\n\nThe git tree id of a directory, computed as git write-tree would over every file below it: entries sorted by git's rule (a directory name compares with a trailing /), modes 100644, 100755, 120000 and 40000, empty directories omitted. The arguments are:\n\ndir — the directory to hash.\nskip — entry names ignored at any level, .git by default, so a working tree hashes the same as the tree its HEAD names when it is clean.\n\nReturns the hex id; an empty tree returns git's well-known empty-tree id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TreeHash.tree_hash"},{"category":"function","location":"api/CLI/#GBGCodingTools.TreeHash.blob_hash","page":"CLI internals","text":"blob_hash(path) -> String\n\nThe git blob id of the file at path: SHA-1 over \"blob <size>\\0\" followed by the content (a symlink hashes its target path, as git does). Returns the 40-character lowercase hex id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TreeHash.blob_hash"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reflection/#Reflection","page":"Reflection","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Reflection"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection","page":"Reflection","text":"Reflection\n\nThe engines of /check-docs that reason about a package's docstrings against its code: argument coverage (Reflection.argcov), the displayed-versus-defined signature table (Reflection.signature_fidelity), unlinked cross-module first mentions (Reflection.xlink), a target linked twice in one docstring (Reflection.dupref), and the ownership-derived public surface (Reflection.package_operations) every completeness guard covers. Two constants of the docstring rubric — docs-rubric.md of the shared policy, its RULE.DOC.* rules — bind the argument checks: the count from which a docstring lists its arguments in the bulleted form (Reflection.ARGS_THRESHOLD), and the phrase that marks a thin allocating wrapper (Reflection.WRAPPER_PHRASE). The text scans need no loaded package; the two reflective ones load it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.argcov","page":"Reflection","text":"argcov(cfg; exempt = (hit -> false)) -> (hits, population, skipped)\n\nThe argument-coverage sweep over the src surface of the config cfg, driven FROM THE SIGNATURE: every positional and keyword parameter of a docstring's displayed signature must appear in its body as a code span, bare (n) or carrying its default (n = 2).\n\nThe arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config, whose src surface names the files read.\nexempt — the GBGCodingTools.Ledger's [exemption.match] predicate, called per trigger as exempt(hit); the trigger population is returned so the doctor can dry-run it.\n\nThe docstring rubric's exemptions are applied, and counted rather than silently dropped: an internal _-prefixed helper; a thin allocating wrapper (the body carries WRAPPER_PHRASE); a docstring with fewer than ARGS_THRESHOLD arguments and no keyword (the prose form, a human read).\n\nReturns the hits, the number of parameters examined, and a named tuple of the skipped counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.argcov"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.signature_fidelity","page":"Reflection","text":"signature_fidelity(cfg; pkg = load_package(cfg),\n                   examined = nothing) -> Vector{String}\n\nPer exported binding, the displayed docstring signatures against the defined method table (positional names accent-normalised, keywords by name): a displayed keyword no method defines (a stale signature), a defined keyword no signature advertises, a displayed positional matching no argument name. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\npkg — the loaded package, loaded from the config when not given.\nexamined — an optional counter the caller passes to learn how many bindings were actually compared, which is the scan's population: a package that exports nothing compares nothing, and a scan that measured nothing must say so rather than print clean.\n\nReturns one message per discrepancy; an extension-homed binding with no methods loaded is reported as skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.signature_fidelity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.xlink","page":"Reflection","text":"xlink(cfg; pkg = load_package(cfg), apply = false) -> Vector\n\nEvery bare mention of a public name owned by ANOTHER package module inside a docstring (the first mention, outside the signature and the arguments), as (file, line, name, owner). The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\npkg — the loaded package (loaded from the config when not given).\napply — rewrite each site to a qualified @ref link in place; default false, a dry run.\n\nReturns the sites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.xlink"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.dupref","page":"Reflection","text":"dupref(cfg) -> Vector\n\nThe @ref targets linked more than once inside one docstring of the src surface (the docstring rubric caps a target at its first genuine mention; invisible in the rendered text). Returns named tuples (file, line, target, count, signature).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.dupref"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.package_operations","page":"Reflection","text":"package_operations(pkg, modules) -> Set{Symbol}\n\nThe derived public surface: every exported function of the umbrella pkg and of its owned modules, plus every external generic a package module extends (found by walking the loaded modules and asking each function whether a package module owns one of its methods — a method defined under a qualified name creates no binding, so a namespace scan cannot see it). Internal helpers and closures never enter it. The arguments are pkg, the umbrella module, and modules, the owned modules. Returns the names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.package_operations"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.WRAPPER_PHRASE","page":"Reflection","text":"WRAPPER_PHRASE\n\nThe docstring rubric's spelling of a thin allocating wrapper — a docstring carrying it documents no arguments, @ref-linking its home instead — which is what makes the exemption detectable rather than a judgement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.WRAPPER_PHRASE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/Reflection/#GBGCodingTools.Reflection.ARGS_THRESHOLD","page":"Reflection","text":"ARGS_THRESHOLD\n\nThe docstring rubric's constant (docs-rubric.md of the shared policy, its RULE.DOC.* rules): a docstring with this many arguments or more, or any keyword, documents them in the bulleted The arguments are: form; fewer are bound in prose. Not a config key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Reflection.ARGS_THRESHOLD"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#GBGCodingTools-—-architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nThis is the target design, and it is normative: code that disagrees with it is wrong, or this document changes first. It follows the owner's rulings; no question about it is open. A term in bold is defined where it first appears, and the reason for each mechanism is given in brackets: a requirement R1–R10 from the owner, or a failure seen in the past. R11 asked for a short normative architecture in plain words: this document is its whole answer. Not in it: the migration order (IMPLEMENTATION.md); history and evidence, with the replaced architecture (docs/design-record/).","title":"GBGCodingTools — architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#1.-Purpose-and-scope","page":"Architecture","text":"GBGCodingTools is a Julia package, on the Julia 1.12 floor. It keeps the owner's Julia packages written to one set of rules, and it helps AI sessions find and fix defects in them without breaking how they work. It serves the four science packages — GBGPeriodicFields, GBGIsotropicTurbulence, GBGStreamingStatistics, GBGCloudPhysics — and itself. Each of the five is a package below; the owner is the person who owns them; a session is one Claude Code conversation working in a package. GBGCodingTools is also called the tooling package here.\n\nIt does four things:\n\nIt holds the rulebook: every rule on how documentation and code are written, each a Julia value carrying its statement, the check that decides a violation and, where a program decides, examples that break and comply [R5, R7].\nIt runs the rules' program checks inside each package's documentation build and test suite, always [R7].\nIt provides instruments: procedures the owner asks a session to run, which find defects. Documentation defects are fixed without the owner and the fixes are checked; code defects are put to the owner [R1, R2].\nIt generates every file that connects a package to GBGCodingTools, so the five carry the same files [R6].\n\nIt is not: needed by anyone who only uses a science package (it is a developer-side dependency, §5.1); a reviewer that runs unasked, apart from the one audit an approved fix carries with it (§4.4); a place for plans or history; and not a defence against a session that forges files. It guards against honest, overconfident mistakes: a program, not a session, decides whether a change is safe and when work stops.\n\nA finding is one reported defect with every place it occurs. A run is one execution of one instrument on one package. An agent is a model instance a session launches to read and report. A failing test is a small test, written by an agent, that fails on the package as it is. Full strength is the most capable model, economy the cheaper default. Every gbg command exits 0 (nothing found), 1 (findings), 2 (cannot evaluate) or 3 (crash); no verdict is read from a log, and no check's output is shortened.","title":"1. Purpose and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#2.-How-the-checks-and-instruments-differ","page":"Architecture","text":"The owner's three distinctions are used, each with one value per check or instrument [R3, R4]:\n\nIntention — what the defects it looks for are in. Documentation: docstrings, comments, hand-written pages and rationale documents, as §4.2 lists exactly. Code: everything else, including stored data that code reads. The future leak scan alone has a third value, publication, because a leaked password is neither [R8].\nDecided by — who says a finding is real. A program; an agent proposes and a program decides (the agent writes a failing test, and the finding exists only if GBGCodingTools runs it and it fails); an agent, quoting it.\nHow found issues are fixed — by whoever ran the build or test, as ordinary work; the documentation fixer (automatic, checked); the code fixer (only on the owner's answer, checked); regenerated; by the owner only.\n\nNo two rows of the table in §3 have the same three values; test/architecture.jl reads those three columns from ALWAYS_ON and INSTRUMENTS and fails on a repeat, and no further distinction is needed to separate them. The last two columns are fields of the same values (§5.2) — an always-on check has no run, so its ending is a dash — and the test compares the whole table. Every rule an agent checks belongs to exactly one agent role, which it checks too.","title":"2. How the checks and instruments differ"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#3.-The-table","page":"Architecture","text":"Always-on checks are programs run by every build and test; nobody starts them and they have no runs [R7]. What they find is fixed by whoever ran that build or test, as part of the work in hand: no run, no verifier, no question. Instruments run only when the owner asks, in words or by slash command (as ruled), with the one exception §10's start rule writes down. The two fixers exist only for what an instrument found. An instrument's name says so: a check is always on, and a review, hunt, audit or scan is an instrument the owner starts; a new one is named the same way. Inside the code the word \"check\" keeps its ordinary meaning (Rule.check, Questions.check, gbg docfix check), and names nothing that runs on its own.\n\n Purpose Intention Decided by How found issues are fixed When it runs A run ends when\nDocumentation checks (always on) Documentation has the house structure and formatting and renders as written Documentation A program Whoever ran it, as ordinary work Every docs build —\nCode checks (always on) The code keeps its tested contracts and every accepted failing test passes Code A program Whoever ran it, as ordinary work Every Pkg.test() —\nWiring check (always on) Every generated file or part of a file equals its generator's output Code A program Regenerated by gbg sync, gbg bump or gbg release Every build and test —\nDocumentation review Documentation is true to the code, correct, consistent and self-contained; the change guard, the checks and a verifier agent check each fix Documentation An agent Documentation fixer Owner's request Nothing is open, or the budget is spent\nBug hunt The code does what its documentation says Code An agent proposes, a program decides Code fixer Owner's request Every candidate is confirmed or discarded, once\nChange audit A code change is no wider than the defect and the owner's direction and still accepts input that worked before; it grades the fix and changes nothing Code An agent Code fixer After each approved code fix, and on request One pass over the change\nLeak scan (future) Nothing secret or personal is published when a repository is made public; it writes nothing but session output Publication A program Owner only Owner's request One pass over every branch, commit and message\n\nA Change audit starts by itself in exactly one place: once, over an approved code fix that has gone green (§4.4) — superseding the earlier ruling that an audit was only advised, since the owner asked to be told not just that a bug was fixed but how well. It never audits its own follow-ups, and §10's start rule carries the exception in full. Documentation review and Bug hunt can meet one disagreement between a docstring and its code from opposite sides: the review compares, the hunt reports only what a failing test shows; either way a possible code defect reaches the owner as a question, and an answered question never returns (§8).\n\nThe leak scan is built later, by adding its rules, a reader of every branch, commit message and Manifest.toml path, one Instrument value and the PUBLICATION intention — none of which exists yet, so the table test (§6) reads that row as the one marked future. It writes no tracked file: no run record, report or work-queue line. Its findings are printed in the session that ran it, where the owner reads them and acts. Guard.refuse_publication is called by every writer of a tracked file — run record, report, question store, settled rulings, work queue — and exits 2 on text from a publication run.","title":"3. The table"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.-Finding-and-fixing","page":"Architecture","text":"FINDING PARTS                        FIXING PARTS\nDocumentation review ─┐              ┌─ about documentation ─► DOCUMENTATION FIXER ─► guarded commit\nBug hunt ─────────────┼─► collect ───┤     ─► checks ─► verifier, blind then with the diff ─► closed or open\nChange audit ─────────┘              └─ about code ─► question ─► the owner's answer, with any direction:\n                                          \"fix the code\" ─► CODE FIXER ─► attempts until green (3 at most) ─► commit\n                                               ─► ONE CHANGE AUDIT ─► its grade and concerns to the owner\n                                          \"fix the documentation instead\" ─► DOCUMENTATION FIXER\n                                          \"not a defect\" ─► optionally recorded as settled (§8)\n\nAn always-on check has no run: a red build or test is fixed by whoever ran it as part of the work in hand, and the wiring check's answer is to regenerate (§3). The two fixers below are for what an instrument found. Finding and fixing are separate parts [R1]: a check or an instrument finds and hands what it found to a fixer, which fixes. What keeps them apart is who does the work — a fix is graded by agents that did not find the defect and are not told how it was fixed (§4.3 step 5), or by an audit that found neither (§4.4). A fix pass is a stage of a run and spends its budget; the judgement of that pass belongs to someone else.\n\nWhy only agent findings are rationed. When a program decides, fixing may repeat until the check passes, with no limit: the check is the same question every time, answers the same way on the same input, and says itself when the work is done. When an agent decides, a fresh reading of edited text is a new opinion, not the same question again, and nothing guarantees it ever reports nothing: hence one search per run, at most four fix passes, and whatever is left carried over [reviews re-reading after each fix ran 9 and 15 passes without settling]. The code fixer's three attempts (§4.4) are not this rationing: they are a judgement about the fixer, not about the loop.","title":"4. Finding and fixing"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.1-Finding,-and-the-handoff","page":"Architecture","text":"Agents write one result file each into a scratch directory and nothing else: collect turns those files into findings and exits 2 when a tracked file changed while agents ran. An agent whose file is missing or malformed is relaunched once, then named in the report. Collect gives each finding an id and fixes the list, so a run never searches twice, and anchors every location (§4.5): the agent gives the file, the quote and, where it knows it, the node; the program fills in the rest, including the line, which it never takes from an agent, and a quote it cannot find is named in the report and given to no fixer. A location in a declared build-time copy (§6) moves to the same node and quote in its tracked source when the copy is identical to it, and is otherwise dropped and reported [fixes landed in a copy the build then overwrote].\n\nA finding goes to the documentation fixer when its instrument's intention is documentation and its agent did not mark it as a possible code defect; otherwise it becomes a question. The change guard has the last word (§4.2).","title":"4.1 Finding, and the handoff"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.2-What-is-documentation,-and-the-change-guard","page":"Architecture","text":"Documentation is exactly: docstrings and comments in .jl files under src/ and ext/; hand-written Markdown under docs/src/; the package's README.md; the rationale documents the config names; and, in GBGCodingTools, the prose of its rules — the statement and why of a Rule, and nothing else in that value. Everything else is code [R2]: other .jl files, whole (tests, docs/make.jl, Literate scripts, whose comment marks decide what runs); Project.toml and Manifest.toml; the config; stored data code reads, in any format; every generated path and declared copy (§6); CI, hook and skill files; CLAUDE.md; any document the config declares normative, which wins over \"rationale\" (this document, for GBGCodingTools) [a fix aligning a specification with code would authorise that code]; and every other part of a rule, agent role or instrument — id, check, examples, parameters, file sets, strength, lists.\n\nThe change guard is the program Guard.classify(cfg, path, before, after, generated), where generated is what that path's generator produces now, or nothing where no generator writes it. The caller supplies it: the guard lives in Engine, which may not reach Render (§5.1), so the verb holding the generators — gbg docfix commit, and the wiring check — regenerates and hands the text in, and no layer below CLI calls a generator [as ruled]. The guard returns documentation only when all of these hold, and code otherwise:\n\na generated path or declared copy whose new content is exactly generated is left out of the classification: it merely follows whatever else the commit changed, and any other content there is code. This is what lets a reworded rule statement be committed with the policy files it feeds (§4.3 step 2);\nthe path is on the documentation list;\nfor .jl: both versions parse; removing line numbers, plain string literals standing alone at top level or in a struct body (unattached and field docstrings), and docstrings whose text is a plain or raw\"…\" literal and whose target is a definition, leaves the same parsed code. A docstring whose target is a call, or a macro call other than a defining one (@kwdef, @enum), makes the change code: Julia then documents the call instead of running it. A docstring with $(…), or a computed @doc argument, is never removed;\nin GBGCodingTools only, differences confined to plain string literals given to the keywords statement or why of a Rule(…) call are documentation — any number of them, in any number of files — with the parsed trees identical at every other node; a moved, added or removed argument, a changed id, check, example or parameter, or an interpolated string in those fields, is code;\nno changed comment contains a Literate mark (#src, #md, #jl, #nb);\nin every removed docstring and every changed page, every fenced block whose info string starts with jldoctest or @ is identical, info string included, and in the same order — doctest input and output, @example, @eval, @setup, @meta, @raw, and the signatures and filters of @docs, @autodocs, @index and @contents.\n\nClause 3 is narrower than \"the prose of a rule and of an agent role\". An agent role's description, like the question of a rule an agent checks, is an instruction to an agent: rewording it changes what the instrument looks for and no program compares it with anything, so it stays code. A rule's statement is read by agents too, so it is not simply documentation either: it is the one code-like text the fixer may touch, and only under §4.3 step 3, listed in the report.\n\nAccepted limit: a longer docstring shifts what @__LINE__ returns — the same shift that moves every line below an edit, which is why a finding is anchored to a node and a quote (§4.5). test/guard.jl holds one example per clause, in both directions [parsed code alone passed changed doctest output, a @autodocs filter, and a docstring that stopped a call from running]. For clause 3: two reworded statements and a reworded why in one file (documentation); a changed check, example, id or argument order, an interpolated statement and a reworded description (code).","title":"4.2 What is documentation, and the change guard"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.3-The-documentation-fixer-—-automatic,-with-checks","page":"Architecture","text":"The documentation fixer is the session at full strength, working through gbg docfix steps. It starts only inside a run the owner asked for, and then fixes and verifies without asking again [R1, R2].\n\nRe-anchor, then other copies. Every pass begins by re-anchoring the open findings (§4.5); a location that comes back OUTDATED is named in the report and not worked on. A program then searches every documentation file for the finding's quote and each formula in it, adding every hit as a further location, anchored like the rest [a fourth copy of a corrected formula survived a fix].\nGuarded commit. The fixer edits, then gbg docfix commit regenerates every generated part whose generator reads the prose that changed — the policy files and the tooling package's block in CLAUDE.md — and classifies every changed path in the working tree with the change guard, which leaves those regenerated parts out by its clause 0. It commits all of them on the current branch if every classified path came back documentation, else exits 1 and commits nothing; the session then pushes. This is the one verb that commits, because the guard must run before a commit exists, replacing the old rule that no verb commits [a code edit committed before the guard ran would be pushed].\nFormula and rule-statement edits. An edit that changes mathematics — inside a math span: a symbol, a number or a sign; years in citations do not count — is made automatically only when the fixer both quotes an expression from the body of the documented method that agrees with the new text and supplies a numeric check: a few lines of Julia, run by the program in scratch, that evaluate the new formula and the method on several inputs drawn across the documented domain, all of which must agree [one input of the fixer's choosing passes where a sign or factor error survives]. Otherwise the finding becomes a question. A reworded rule statement (§4.2 clause 3) is treated the same way, because no program compares a statement with the check it names. Both kinds are verified blind at full strength — the verifier of a statement is given the rule's check and its examples — and listed in the report [a docstring was \"fixed\" to contradict code that was itself wrong; a fix wrote false mathematics].\nChecks. gbg docfix check runs the documentation checks, doctests and docs build; a check that passed at the run's first commit and fails now is exit 1.\nVerifier, blind first. gbg run next re-anchors the findings (§4.5) and writes the blind stage's arguments from the anchors it got, so the text a verifier is sent is the text the finding is about. A verifier reads, in a copy of the tree exported without git history, each finding, the current text at its locations — or, for an OUTDATED one, the file's changed regions — and of every region the pass changed, and the code that text documents, without the old text or the fixer's explanation. It answers FIXED, NOT_FIXED, HIDES_CODE_DEFECT (the documentation describes code that looks wrong) or NOT_A_DEFECT. Only after that stage is collected does gbg run next write the second, for FIXED findings only, which sees the diff and may answer LINE_ONLY (fixed at some locations only) or MADE_WORSE [blind reading caught false mathematics; the diff stage caught 11 of 13 fixes made at one line only].\nGrade. A program applies the verdicts. Every verdict except NOT_FIXED needs a quote the program finds in the current text, or it becomes NOT_FIXED. MADE_WORSE reverts the edit; HIDES_CODE_DEFECT reverts it and asks the owner — unless the owner already answered \"fix the documentation instead\", when the edit stands and is listed. A NOT_A_DEFECT closes the finding for this run, listed but never stored.\nPasses. Open findings return to step 1, up to 4 [verified fixes rose 16 → 39 → 55 → 74 % over four passes in one package, 43 → 69 → 76 → 95 % in another, then 93 and 90].","title":"4.3 The documentation fixer — automatic, with checks"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.4-The-code-fixer-—-only-on-the-owner's-answer","page":"Architecture","text":"The code fixer is the session making a code change the owner asked for [R2].\n\nApproval, and direction. A code finding becomes an item of a question (§8) and nothing is touched until the owner answers \"fix the code\"; a run's code findings form one question with an answer per item [a hunt confirms 9 to 21 defects]. The answer may carry the owner's direction — how he wants it fixed — recorded with the question, handed to the fixer, and handed to the audit as part of what the change is measured against.\nAttempts, on the deterministic oracle. The fix then repeats until the failing test goes from failing to passing and Pkg.test() and the docs build are green; it may loop because the oracle is the same question every time and answers the same way (§4), so it ends by passing. Three guards: the failing test is frozen for the loop and an attempt that edits it does not count [weakening the test is the cheapest way to pass]; any change under test/ is named first to the audit [a fix that also edits the test that would have caught it is the shape an audit exists for]; and after three failed attempts the loop stops and the attempts, with what each failed on, go to the owner as a question [in the owner's words: if the same problem is not fixed in three attempts, there is reason to believe the fixer cannot fix it cleanly — a judgement about the fixer, not the budget of §4].\nCommit. The change is committed on the current branch and pushed; there is no separate branch. The failing test of a question the owner has not yet answered is committed by gbg run close into test/regressions/escalated/ — the one code path a run may add to, since nothing there is run or referenced and a witness left in scratch is lost. Pkg.test() never runs that directory; on a green fix the code fixer moves the test unchanged into test/regressions/, and on any other answer deletes it.\nOne audit, and a grade. A Change audit then runs once over that change, without the owner asking. It is given the finding, the owner's direction and the diff, and returns concerns and a grade: whether the change was no wider than the defect and that direction, whether it changed a default or a documented contract, and whether input that worked before still works [an audit caught two regressions the failing tests missed]. Both reach the owner with the fix, in the CodeFix kept with its question (§5.2) — in the run record when the question came from a run, in the question store otherwise — and in the run's report: not only that it is fixed, but how well.\nNo recursion. The audit never audits its own follow-ups: a concern the owner wants fixed is a new question, and on his approval it loops as above and gets its own single audit. Nothing chains on its own and every step waits on an answer [fix → audit → fix, each audit fixed inside the next, is the loop that had to be stopped].","title":"4.4 The code fixer — only on the owner's answer"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.5-Where-a-finding-is,-while-the-text-moves","page":"Architecture","text":"A line number is not a place: a fix earlier in a file moves every line after it, four passes compound that, and a carried finding's lines were read at an older commit, so a stale number points at whatever now sits there — which is how a fix lands in the wrong paragraph. So a location (§5.2) is the file; the node — for .jl the binding or method documented, or the field of a constant when the text is a generated docstring (ARGS_FORM.statement), for Markdown the heading path, empty where there is none of these; the quote, the decisive text as it read then; and the line, kept only as a hint.\n\nRe-anchoring runs before each fix pass, before each verifier stage, and when a finding is carried into a later run. Texts.reanchor does the two steps that need only files: the node — parse the file with Julia's own parser (JuliaSyntax, part of Julia 1.12) and find the named definition, or scan the Markdown headings — and then the quote inside it (BY_NODE); failing that, the quote alone, nearest the old line when there are several (BY_QUOTE). Runs.reanchor is the wrapper a run calls: it tries Texts.reanchor first and, when that fails, maps the old line by git through the changes since the finding was made (BY_DIFF) — which is why the git step lives there, not in Texts (§5.1). If all three fail the location is OUTDATED, never pointed at whatever now sits on that line: the report names it, the fixer is not sent there, and a verifier that needs it is given the file's changed regions.","title":"4.5 Where a finding is, while the text moves"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.-The-Julia-object-model","page":"Architecture","text":"","title":"5. The Julia object model"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.1-Modules,-types-first","page":"Architecture","text":"Each module uses only the modules above it. A rule's check may use only Model and Texts, which is why rules sit beside their checks in Catalogue, above the machinery that runs them. What executes inside a consumer's build or test — the harness and the makedocs wrapper — may not reach Orchestration, where run records live; the tooling package's own test files may, since they test it. test/architecture.jl parses the sources and fails on any reference, qualified or imported, that breaks either rule.\n\nModel          every struct and enum; no behaviour, no values\nTexts          reading docstrings, pages and the built site, given a Config value; anchoring by node and quote\nCatalogue      RULES (src/Rules/*.jl, each rule beside its checks), INSTRUMENTS, ALWAYS_ON, roles, GENERATED — data\nConfigIO       the literal-only readers of config.jl and settled.jl; validate; applies\nEngine         Checks, Harness, Guard, Questions.check's programs   ← the harness reaches no further\nRender         the generators and the wiring check                  ← the makedocs wrapper reaches no further\nOrchestration  Agents (arguments), Runs, and Runs.reanchor's git step\nCLI            the gbg verbs, each a function returning an exit code\n\nWhat must be Julia, and what may be borrowed. The skeleton is Julia because of what it is for: the types, the rules with their checks and examples, the agent roles, the instruments and the generators are what the owner reads, what Documenter renders on the package's own site, and what its own instruments read when pointed at it; a rulebook in another language could be neither reviewed as values nor checked by the machinery it describes. Beyond the skeleton there is no thrift: packages and external programs are used wherever they do the job better, which replaces the older rule of no Python and no child process beyond a few named commands, still carried in CLAUDE.md [as ruled].\n\nOne boundary stays: whose dependency this is. GBGCodingTools is a developer-side dependency: it lives in each package's docs/ and test/ environments, which gbg sync writes, and never in the package's own [deps], so ]add GBGPeriodicFields pulls none of it and no user of a science package resolves it. What it brings — JSON3, Gumbo and the rest — is paid for there, by the developer. What runs inside a consumer's build or test stays offline and starts no child process, because CI must give the same answer every time; the instrument side, run only when the owner asks, is free, and already starts git, the docs build, the tests and witnesses. Settled case by case:\n\nJob Answer Reason, and what it costs\nThe Workflow driver one fixed JavaScript file, checked by a fixture launch the Workflow tool runs JavaScript and nothing else; no JavaScript parser, since node is not installed here, so a driver it cannot parse shows up as exit 2\nReading config.jl and settled.jl; the Markdown heading path hand-rolled: Julia's parser plus forty lines of \"these literals only\"; ten lines of heading scan a parser package would be no shorter, a sandbox to evaluate in reopens the hole the reader closes, and Julia's Markdown standard library drops the line an anchor needs\nSearching text Julia 627 Markdown and Julia files of a science package, 8.6 MB, in 60 ms; ripgrep is not installed here\nRecords, agent schemas, the built site JSON3 and Gumbo 100 ms and 5 ms to load, once per process, against writing a parser and an HTML reader; both are paid for in the developer's docs/ and test/ environments, never in a user's\nDiffs, hashes, history git, an external program every workflow here needs it already, and a diff reimplemented in Julia would be strictly worse","title":"5.1 Modules, types first"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.2-Types","page":"Architecture","text":"Enums are scoped (module Decider; @enum T …; end) so values of different enums never share a name.\n\nmodule Intention; @enum T DOCUMENTATION CODE; end          # PUBLICATION arrives with the leak scan\nmodule Decider;   @enum T PROGRAM FAILING_TEST AGENT; end\nmodule Fixer;     @enum T ORDINARY_WORK DOCUMENTATION_FIXER CODE_FIXER REGENERATE OWNER_ONLY; end\nmodule Input;     @enum T SOURCE_TEXT FILE_TREE LOADED_MODULE BUILT_SITE QUESTION; end\nmodule FileSet;   @enum T DOCSTRINGS PAGES RATIONALE LITERATE CODE TESTS; end\nmodule Strength;  @enum T ECONOMY FULL; end; module Part; @enum T WHOLE BLOCK TOML_ENTRIES; end\nmodule Verdict;   @enum T FIXED NOT_FIXED LINE_ONLY MADE_WORSE HIDES_CODE_DEFECT NOT_A_DEFECT; end\nmodule Stop;      @enum T DONE BUDGET_SPENT CANNOT_EVALUATE OWNER_STOPPED; end\nmodule WhyAgent;  @enum T MEANING DERIVATION READING_ORDER; end\nmodule Anchor;    @enum T FRESH BY_NODE BY_QUOTE BY_DIFF OUTDATED; end\n\"Where a finding is: the node and the quote hold it still while the text around it moves; the line is a hint (§4.5).\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Location       # node: for `.jl` the binding, method or field of a constant documented\n    file::String; node::String = \"\"; text::String = \"\"   # (`ARGS_FORM.statement`), for `.md` the heading path;\n    line::Int = 0; found::Anchor.T = Anchor.FRESH        # text: the quote. The line is a hint, `found` its anchoring\nend\n\"What an agent or a check reports, before a run gives it an id.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct FindingDraft\n    rule::Union{Symbol,Nothing}; locations::Vector{Location}   # every site, each with its own quote\n    claim::String; evidence::String; fix::String               # evidence: a failing test's path, or a reason\n    maybe_code::Bool = false                                   # the agent thinks the code, not the text, is wrong\nend\n\"One defect in a run: the draft, its id, and who reported it.\"\nstruct Finding; id::String; source::Symbol; decider::Decider.T; draft::FindingDraft; end\n\nA file set is a named set of a package's files, found by default patterns the config may override. A check's input is what it reads: a source text, a small file tree, the loaded package, the built site, or a question.\n\n\"A possible answer, with what happens if it is chosen and what it costs.\"\nstruct Option; label::String; consequence::String; recommended::Bool; end\n\"One decision inside a question; items share the question's options, and each is answered on its own.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Item\n    text::String; recommended::String; sources::Vector{String}\n    answer::Union{String,Nothing} = nothing; direction::String = \"\"   # this item's answer, and any direction with it\nend\n\"A decision put to the owner; plain data, checked only when it is put.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Question\n    id::String; decision::String; context::String; options::Vector{Option}; items::Vector{Item} = Item[]\n    exhibit::String = \"\"              # commits, changed files and definitions: exempt from the language rules\n    answer::Union{String,Nothing} = nothing; direction::String = \"\"   # for a question with no items (§4.4)\nend\n\"What decides a violation: a program, or an agent for one of the reasons in WhyAgent.\"\nabstract type Check end\nstruct ProgramCheck <: Check; input::Input.T; run::Function; network::Bool; end  # run(x, cfg)::Vector{Location}\n# it returns file and line, adding node and quote where its parse already holds them; re-run from scratch, it never\n# needs re-anchoring. `network = true` keeps it out of every build and test: only `gbg check --network` runs it (§11)\nstruct AgentCheck   <: Check; question::String; why::WhyAgent.T; end\n\"Something a rule's check must judge a known way; `expect` lists the locations it must report, empty if it complies.\nAn example's locations carry file and line only: a fixture is built fresh each run, so nothing can drift under it.\"\nabstract type Example end\nstruct TextExample     <: Example; fileset::FileSet.T; text::String; expect::Vector{Location}; clause::String; end\nstruct TreeExample     <: Example; files::Vector{Pair{String,String}}; expect::Vector{Location}; clause::String; end\nstruct ModuleExample   <: Example; source::String; expect::Vector{Location}; clause::String; end\nstruct SiteExample     <: Example; pages::Vector{Pair{String,String}}; expect::Vector{Location}; clause::String; end\nstruct QuestionExample <: Example; question::Question; expect::Vector{Location}; clause::String; end\n\"A value a rule reads that a package may set, such as how a docstring's first sentence leads.\"\nstruct Parameter{T}; name::Symbol; default::T; end\n\"One rule of the rulebook.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Rule\n    id::Symbol; statement::String; why::String   # statement: the rule in plain English, its only home\n    intention::Intention.T; filesets::Vector{FileSet.T}   # documentation rules run in the docs build, code rules in\n    check::Union{ProgramCheck,AgentCheck,Nothing}         # Pkg.test(); one whose input is QUESTION in neither (§8)\n    unchecked_reason::String = \"\"; parameters::Vector{Parameter} = Parameter[]  # reason: required with no check\n    examples::Vector{Example} = Example[]      # a program check needs them; a rule an agent checks needs none\nend\n\ntest/rules.jl materialises every example — a text; a temporary file tree; a source string loaded into a fresh module; all site examples in one Documenter build with warnings allowed, each page in its own directory (a strict build writes nothing once one example breaks) — and requires the check's locations to equal expect in file and line. For a program check, every clause of the statement needs a breaking example and a complying text that differs from a breaking one as little as possible [a yes/no example passes a check that always fires]; a rule an agent checks needs none.\n\n\"One kind of agent an instrument launches: what it reads, what it is kept from, and which rules it checks.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct AgentRole\n    id::Symbol; instrument::Symbol; reads::Vector{FileSet.T}; site::Bool = false; kept_from_code::Bool = false\n    description::String                  # who the reader is, in two or three sentences\n    rules::Vector{Symbol}; strength::Strength.T = Strength.ECONOMY  # rule ids; empty makes an open reader\n    cap::Int   # distinct defects reported, the agent saying how many it left out [readers were cut off at the limit]\nend\n\"The ceiling on one run; reaching any part of it ends the run.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Budget; fix_passes::Int = 4; agent_launches::Int; end\n\"An instrument the owner starts, with its three values from §2 and the two prose columns of that table.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Instrument\n    id::Symbol; purpose::String; intention::Intention.T; decider::Decider.T; fixer::Fixer.T\n    when::String; ends::String                  # the last two columns of §3, in the same words\n    roles::Vector{AgentRole}; budget::Budget\nend\n\"A program check that every build and test runs; its decider is a program by definition.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct AlwaysOn\n    id::Symbol; purpose::String; intention::Intention.T; fixer::Fixer.T\n    decider::Decider.T = Decider.PROGRAM; when::String\nend\n\"Generated files or parts of files: which paths for a package, which part, and which generator writes it.\"\nstruct Generated; paths::Function; part::Part.T; generator::Symbol; end   # paths(cfg)::Vector{String}\n\nAn open reader is an agent role with no rule list, looking for what no rule names yet; its findings name no rule, so the reason for trusting an agent there is the role's own description rather than a WhyAgent value, and a finding that recurs becomes a rule (§7) [in the past, unlisted readers found 9 defects at a third of the cost]. Documentation review has two groups of roles: code readers, which read each docstring or page with the code it describes and own rules such as \"the formula matches the code\"; and site readers, which read the built site as text with the code kept from them and own rules such as \"the derivation is right\" — and those checking mathematics run at full strength in science packages [in one past round two such readers found 4 planted defects each, where ten economy readers found 2 between them]. A hat and a lane are both agent roles.\n\n\"Turns one rule, agent role or instrument off in one package, or in some of its files, with the reason.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Switch; target::Symbol; reason::String; files::Vector{String} = String[]; end\n\"A package's value for one rule parameter; a list-valued parameter is set the same way.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Setting; rule::Symbol; name::Symbol; value::Union{String,Symbol,Bool,Int,Vector{String}}; end\n\"A rule a package adds for itself: a pattern its files must not (or must) contain, with examples.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct PatternRule\n    id::Symbol; statement::String; fileset::FileSet.T; pattern::Regex; forbidden::Bool; examples::Vector{TextExample}\nend\n\"A package's settings, written by hand in `.gbg/config.jl`.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Config\n    package::String; tool::Union{VersionNumber,Symbol}   # a released version, or :dev in GBGCodingTools' own checkout\n    docs_build::Vector{String}; test::Vector{String}; rules::Vector{PatternRule} = PatternRule[]\n    filesets::Dict{FileSet.T,Vector{String}} = Dict{FileSet.T,Vector{String}}(); normative::Vector{String} = String[]\n    copies::Vector{Pair{String,String}} = Pair{String,String}[]  # build-time copy => tracked source\n    switches::Vector{Switch} = Switch[]; settings::Vector{Setting} = Setting[]\n    roles::Vector{AgentRole} = AgentRole[]                      # the package's own expert readers\n    code_areas::Vector{Pair{Symbol,Vector{String}}} = Pair{Symbol,Vector{String}}[]  # one hunter per area (§10)\nend\n\"A question the owner settled, kept in `.gbg/settled.jl` and given to agents as 'do not report'.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Settled                        # locations: what the ruling is about, quotes included\n    question::String; text::String; date::String; locations::Vector{Location}   # date as \"YYYY-MM-DD\"\nend\n\n.gbg/config.jl and .gbg/settled.jl are never evaluated (§5.1): ConfigIO parses them and accepts only literals (strings, numbers, symbols, enum names, v\"…\", r\"…\", vectors, pairs, dictionaries) and keyword calls to the constructors above; a function call, interpolation, unknown keyword or unknown id is exit 2 [a module holding only the config type still ran getpid() through it]. A test round-trips every field type; a package rule needing a function lives in the package's own tests as a Rule passed to Harness.test; new rulings are appended as text.\n\n\"One documentation-fixer attempt at one finding, with the program and agent judgements of it.\"\nBase.@kwdef struct DocFix\n    finding::String; pass::Int; commit::String; formula_edit::Bool\n    blind::Verdict.T; with_diff::Union{Verdict.T,Nothing} = nothing  # the blind verdict (§4.3 step 5), then the\n                                                                     # diff stage's, for a FIXED finding only\n    agreeing_code::Union{Location,Nothing} = nothing  # anchored like any other: its quote is the code line itself\nend\n\"One approved code fix and the single audit of it; kept with its question (§4.4).\"\nBase.@kwdef struct CodeFix\n    question::String; direction::String = \"\"   # the answer that approved it, and any direction it carried\n    attempts::Int; commits::Vector{String}; touched_tests::Bool; audit::String   # audit: the one run over it\n    proportionate::Bool; contract_kept::Bool; old_input_works::Bool   # the grade the audit returned\n    concerns::Vector{String}; note::String     # its concerns, and its sentence to the owner\nend\n\"One run, stored beside report.md as .gbg/runs/<id>/run.json through JSON3 (enums and `nothing` round-trip).\"\nBase.@kwdef struct Run\n    id::String; instrument::Symbol; tool_version::String; first_commit::String; budget::Budget; stage::Symbol\n    findings::Vector{Finding}; fixes::Vector{DocFix}; questions::Vector{Question}\n    code_fixes::Vector{CodeFix} = CodeFix[]   # the fixes its questions were answered with, added as they land\n    agent_launches::Int; tokens::Union{Int,Nothing}; stop::Stop.T\nend\n\nRule docstrings. Each Rule, AgentRole and Instrument constant gets its docstring from doc(x), attached by a @doc doc(x) x call in the source. The rule that source docstrings match the loaded module's compares only literal docstrings, and requires every other docstring in the module to come from such a call, found in the source. Each example is fenced with one more backtick than the longest run inside it, so its own fences and doctests stay inert. doc(x) also returns which field each docstring line came from, located with Julia's own parser, so a finding in such a docstring anchors to that field — node ARGS_FORM.statement, say — not to a line of a text no file holds. The generated pages showing the rules stay outside the file set the site readers get, and the generated policy files carry statements without examples, because a rules page is by construction full of text that breaks the rules, which a reader would spend its budget reporting and no fixer could touch.","title":"5.2 Types"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.3-What-becomes-Julia","page":"Architecture","text":"The rule prose, the rule register and the scan catalogue become Rule values beside their checks, the fixture's defects their Examples; hats, lanes and the model catalogue become AgentRole values, their defect lists agent-checked rules; instrument protocols and Workflow scripts become Instrument values, Runs stages and one fixed driver; JSON schemas become Julia constructors, with agents' output schemas generated from the types; config.toml and the exemptions ledger both become config.jl, an exemption being a Switch there, while settled.jl is new and holds only the owner's rulings; templates and managed.json become Render generators and the GENERATED registry.","title":"5.3 What becomes Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#6.-Generation","page":"Architecture","text":"Every non-Julia file that a program or an agent reads as data is written by a Julia function [R6]. Prose written by a person for people — README.md, the pages, this document, CHANGELOG.md — is written by hand, and an agent reads it as the text under review; a table in such prose restating Julia values is compared with them by a test, as §3's table is, with the future leak-scan row read as future.\n\nFile, or part of a file Read by Written by\n.gbg/policy/*.md (statements, no examples); the tooling package's block in CLAUDE.md sessions Render.policy\none .claude/skills/<instrument>/SKILL.md per instrument that applies Claude Code Render.skill\nthe Workflow driver, agents' arguments and output schemas (scratch) the Workflow tool, agents Agents\nthe pin in docs/Project.toml and test/Project.toml Pkg Render.pin\nthe tooling package's block in .gitignore, the CI workflow files, the end-of-turn hook git, GitHub, Claude Code Render.gitignore, Render.ci, Render.hook\nthe rules, instruments and switches pages of each site readers Render.pages, called by docs/make.jl\n\nCatalogue.GENERATED lists every generated path or part — a whole file, a marked block, or the entries of a TOML table — with its generator; a package's declared copies count as generated. Run records, reports and the question store are not in it: they record what happened, written by Runs and Render.report, and nothing regenerates or compares them. The wiring check regenerates each part and compares only that part, TOML entries as parsed values since Pkg rewrites the files [a header line cannot mark a JSON or skill file, and comparing all of CLAUDE.md always fails]. Because a generated part equal to its generator's output is not a code change, gbg docfix commit may regenerate and commit the parts a reworded rule statement feeds (§4.3), so a prose fix never leaves that check red.\n\nThe Workflow driver is one fixed script: it launches the agents its arguments list and reports which wrote their files. Julia cannot launch agents, and only the session can call the Workflow tool, so each stage is a handoff: gbg run next writes the stage's arguments and exits, the session runs the Workflow, gbg run collect reads the results, and Runs refuses any stage out of order. A Julia test checks the driver reads every key Agents writes. An agent's output schema asks, for each place, the file, the quote and the node it read; the program fills in the line itself (§4.1).","title":"6. Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#7.-Deterministic-first","page":"Architecture","text":"When an agent may check a rule [R7]. Only when deciding it needs the meaning of a sentence (is this claim true of the code), a derivation (is this formula right), or a document read in order (is this term defined before its first use) — the three WhyAgent values. Whatever a pattern, a parser, reflection on the loaded package or the Documenter build can decide is a ProgramCheck from the start; that includes a docstring's stated defaults and argument names against the signature, and docstrings in the source attached to nothing [five such went unfound]. A partly decidable rule splits into a program rule and an agent rule; a rule with no check states why in unchecked_reason and is listed as unchecked on the rules page.\n\nA check is shown to match its rule by test/rules.jl (§5.2). Bug hunt can also be pointed at the rules, where a failing test is a text the rule forbids but its check lets pass, or the reverse; only the owner decides what a rule means, so each becomes a question, and on \"yes\" the text becomes an example and the code fixer changes the check.\n\nA recurring agent finding becomes a program check. Each verified documentation fix leaves its commit — the text before, which broke the rule, and after, which complies — in the run record, and the report lists the fixes per agent-checked rule. When a rule recurs, the session proposes a program check as a code change; those pairs are its first examples, and test/rules.jl must pass on them.","title":"7. Deterministic first"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#8.-Asking-the-owner","page":"Architecture","text":"Question is the one form for asking a person anything [R9]: the documentation fixer's (a possible code defect; a formula edit without agreement and a numeric check), Bug hunt's, Change audit's concerns, the code fixer's approvals and the attempts that hit its cap, the keep-or-revert a run's close puts when code changed outside the guarded commit (§10), rule counterexamples, and proposed program checks. The wiring check regenerates, or exits 2 when it cannot read the config, and the session then asks through a Question kept outside a run.\n\nHow the language rules are applied. Questions.check(q, cfg) runs whenever a question is put, inside a run or out of one, never when a record is read [an older record would fail to load under a newer rule]. It selects, at that moment, every program rule in RULES whose input is a question, so no rule text is copied and a changed rule applies to the next question. These check, for example: a decision of one sentence ending in a question mark; a length limit; no rule id, finding id, file path or code name in the decision or context (those go in items and exhibit); no word on the rule's list of the tooling package's own terms (a Parameter). The agent-checked language rules — plain, self-contained, no invented jargon — are judged by one agent before questions are shown. A failing question is rewritten once, then shown with the failed rule named; report.md ends with the rendered questions, and test/questions.jl checks every question form built.\n\nAnswers. gbg answer <question> [item] <option> [direction] records the answer, and any direction with it, on the question or the named item: in the run record for a run's questions, otherwise in .gbg/questions.json. Each question's CodeFix (§4.4) is written beside it in the same place. An answered question never returns: grading and later runs skip any finding whose question has an answer, unless the answer was \"fix the documentation instead\" — that finding waits for the next run of its instrument, where the documentation fixer takes it. An owner's \"not a defect, do not ask again\" appends a Settled value to .gbg/settled.jl, tied to the question id and holding the anchored locations it covers. ConfigIO parses that file and Agents is its only reader, telling agents \"do not report\" while each of those locations still re-anchors (§4.5); no build, test or check reads it [settled choices were raised again in 4 of 87 findings].","title":"8. Asking the owner"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#9.-Applicability","page":"Architecture","text":"A package turns a rule, an agent role or a whole instrument off with a Switch naming it and giving the reason, and sets a variant with a Setting [R10]. Every element that is off is listed in the config and shown, with its reason, on the package's documentation site; an unknown target, a missing reason, or a switch against a rule the rulebook marks as always on is exit 2. Those always-on rules are the ones catching text that renders wrongly (a stray $, a formula in backticks) and the docstring-structure rules; a test builds a page with such a fault under GBGCodingTools' own switches and requires a failed build.\n\nThat mark is not set from the start. Until two packages have run those rules over their own prose and their counts are known, the set ships switchable, so a rule that proves wrong for one package's writing is switched off there with its reason while it is fixed or dropped [a rule nobody may escape is settled before anyone has read what it reports]. Setting the mark is a MAJOR release; after it the only escape is changing the rule for every package, through §13 [as ruled].\n\nOff in GBGCodingTools, with reasons — the second and third are its declared deviations today, the first and fourth new:\n\nthe LaTeX notation and spacing rules, the citation rules, and the site readers checking mathematics, notation, typesetting and citations — its documentation contains no mathematics and cites no literature;\nthe allocation and memory-traffic contracts — no operation here writes into array storage whose traffic matters;\nrunning Literate examples — it ships none;\nthe numerical and device-portability roles of Bug hunt and Change audit — no numerical kernels or device arrays.\n\nSettings here: a docstring's first sentence says what the operation does (science packages: the physical meaning of what it computes); the \"written by AI\" banner is on every page.","title":"9. Applicability"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#10.-How-a-run-starts,-works-and-stops","page":"Architecture","text":"Start. A run starts only on the owner's request, in words or by slash command (as ruled), with one exception: the single Change audit of an approved code fix (§4.4), which the owner's \"fix the code\" authorises with the fix and which starts nothing further. Nothing else — no gate, no check, no other fix, no other run — starts a run [in the past, 8 of 15 rounds here were opened by the session or by a gate]. Once started, a run fixes and verifies to its end without asking again. gbg run start <instrument> exits 2 when the working tree is not clean or another run is open, and records the version of GBGCodingTools, the first commit and the budget.\n\nCarried findings, and the two places they live. Open findings carry over, as ruled. The run record keeps them with their ids and anchored locations: the machine's copy, which the next run of that instrument starts from, before its own search. That next run re-anchors each one first (§4.5); what comes back OUTDATED — the text it was about is gone or rewritten past recognition — is closed as no longer locatable and said so in the report, not carried for ever. At close, every instrument but the leak scan also writes one line per leftover into .claude/WORK_QUEUE.md, the owner's list — a review's unfixed findings, a hunt's confirmed defects whose question is unanswered, an audit's concerns — each naming the file, the node and the quote rather than a line number, stale by the time the owner reads it. Those lines go under a heading the end-of-turn guard does not count, so the list never makes a session work on something the owner has not asked about [a session held to that list could only edit by hand or open a run nobody asked for]. The report keeps no second list of leftovers: it names the run record and says how many lines went to the queue.\n\nWork. Runs orders the stages; the session launches each one (§6). Documentation review: start → find → collect → up to 4 passes (§4.3) → close. Bug hunt: start → hunt, one hunter per code area of the config and the open readers → collect → agents write failing tests → gbg run witnesses runs them → collect → close. Change audit: start → audit → collect → close, the package's named tests run as evidence. gbg run close writes run.json and report.md — for a leak scan, neither (§3). The session commits and pushes what was written, shows the report and questions, and ends.\n\nEnd, decided by a program and recorded in Run.stop, whatever the §3 table's ordinary ending says: DONE; BUDGET_SPENT (fix passes or agent launches used up; the open findings go to the record and the queue); CANNOT_EVALUATE (a step exited 2 or 3; the instrument is not repaired inside the run, which rounds that patched their own scripts showed the need of); OWNER_STOPPED. Close also runs two programs:\n\nit classifies every commit since the run's first commit; if any changed code outside gbg docfix commit, the run ends CANNOT_EVALUATE and the owner is asked to keep or revert it, with an exhibit the program writes;\nit runs gbg docfix check; if the build is red, it restores the documentation to the last commit that passed and commits that through gbg docfix commit [a red docs build would otherwise stay on the branch].\n\nA run never searches twice, extends its budget, commits code outside test/regressions/escalated/ (§4.4 step 3), starts another run, or changes an instrument (instruments are code); discarded bug-hunt candidates are listed with their passing tests [one was a real blocker].\n\nAcross runs. No build, test or wiring check reads a run record, the question store or the settled rulings [review state turned science tests red]. The work-queue file and the end-of-turn guard that holds a session to it stay as ruled; a run's close is the only verb writing to that file, one line per leftover, under the uncounted heading.\n\nRelease of GBGCodingTools needs a clean tree on master, pushed; a green Pkg.test(), a green docs build and a green wiring check; and the CHANGELOG.md entry; the tag is never moved. gbg release prints, and the tag message and that entry carry, two lines it does not act on: the commits whose keep-or-revert question is still open, and how many confirmed failing tests wait unanswered in test/regressions/escalated/. Nothing else holds a release: no review run, no open finding, no unanswered question, no audit.\n\nWho stops. Inside a run, the program, from the budget fixed at start; across runs, the owner, by asking or not. A program-decided loop needs no budget: it ends itself by passing (§4); the code fixer's three attempts are a judgement about the fixer, not a budget (§4.4 step 2).\n\nMechanism The failure it prevents Its cost\nOne search per run; the finding list fixed at collect Repeated searches never settled A defect the search missed waits for the next run\nAt most 4 fix passes Rounds of 9 and 15 passes Findings open after 4 passes carry over\nCarried findings, in the record and the work queue A leftover forgotten between reviews The next review starts with the last one's leftovers\nLocations anchored to a node and a quote, re-anchored before each use A fix or a verifier sent to a line that has since moved A finding whose text is gone closes as no longer locatable\nGuarded commit; classification and build check at close Code changed, or a red build pushed, through a documentation fix A documentation fix needing code waits for the owner\nCode findings end as questions Audit verdicts drove code changes without review Owner attention per code change\nAttempts loop on the failing test, at most 3 A fix nobody can find turning into a speculative rewrite After three failures the owner reads the attempts and decides\nOne audit after each approved fix, never after itself Fix → audit → fix, the chain the owner was lost in One audit's agent time per approved fix\nNothing outside the run machinery reads run records Review state turned science tests red, and held releases What is owed is read from the work queue, not enforced\nA crashed step ends the run Abandoned rounds; scripts replaced mid-round That run's agent launches are lost","title":"10. How a run starts, works and stops"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#11.-How-GBGCodingTools-plugs-into-a-package","page":"Architecture","text":"The pinned version. Config.tool names one released version (as ruled) — :dev only in the tooling package's own checkout — which gbg sync writes into the docs and test environments, and into neither package's own dependencies. gbg bump runs only on the owner's request; it first runs the new version's checks on the package and exits 1, changing nothing, with counts per new rule when any fails; else it changes the pin and regenerates every generated part [a new check would otherwise redden a build on old text].\n\nInside a science package's Pkg.test() the code rules run and the wiring check compares, and inside its docs build the documentation rules run and the wiring check compares again; regenerating anything is left to gbg sync, gbg bump and gbg release, so a build only reports what has drifted. GBGCodingTools' own test suite runs more, being the tooling package's: test/rules.jl (which builds one fixture site), test/guard.jl, test/anchors.jl, test/questions.jl and test/architecture.jl.\n\nGBGCodingTools.Harness.test(MyPackage; rules = MY_RULES)      # test/runtests.jl: contracts, accepted failing tests\nGBGCodingTools.makedocs(GBGCodingTools.ConfigIO.read_config(path); sitename = \"MyPackage\", pages = [...])\n\nThe package's own preparation (Literate output, copied rationale pages) runs in docs/make.jl before makedocs; its outputs are declared copies or generated paths. makedocs runs the documentation rules over the source, then Documenter.makedocs, then the rules over the built site, and throws on any finding. Both entry points are deterministic and offline, agree on a fresh clone and on CI, and never read run records, questions, settled rulings or git state. A used file set matching no file is exit 2, and literal docstrings in the source must match the loaded module's [a check with no input reports clean].\n\nThe same files in every package, including GBGCodingTools, created by gbg init:\n\n.gbg/config.jl               written by hand\n.gbg/settled.jl              the owner's settled rulings, appended on \"do not ask again\"\n.gbg/questions.json          questions put outside a run, with their answers\n.gbg/policy/*.md             generated\n.gbg/runs/<id>/              run.json and report.md\n.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md    generated, one per instrument that applies\n.claude/WORK_QUEUE.md        the owner's items, guarded; under an unguarded heading, each run's leftovers\n.claude/settings.json        the end-of-turn hook, generated\nCLAUDE.md                    the tooling package's block generated; the rest belongs to the package\ndocs/, test/Project.toml     the pin, generated into both environments\n.gitignore, CI workflows     the tooling package's block and its workflow files, generated\ntest/regressions/            accepted failing tests; escalated/ holds those awaiting an answer\n\nAgent output and the driver live in a scratch directory that git ignores.\n\ngbg init never overwrites what the package wrote itself. Four of those paths are commonly a package's own before the tooling arrives — a CI workflow, .claude/settings.json, the work queue, and a skill whose name is already taken by one of the package's commands. Where a file exists and did not come from a generator, init writes everything else, prints the difference for each collision and exits 1; the owner moves or keeps his own file and runs it again [a generated file silently replacing a hand-written workflow is the one wiring mistake that is noticed late]. Unwiring a package is reverting the commits that wired it, after which gbg finds no config there and refuses.","title":"11. How GBGCodingTools plugs into a package"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#12.-What-is-shown-to-work,-and-what-is-not","page":"Architecture","text":"Program checks are shown to work by their examples (§5.2): every check is run on every example of its rule, and its locations must equal what the example expects, in both directions and for every clause. Re-anchoring is shown the same way, by test/anchors.jl, which uses Runs.reanchor and so is one of the tooling package's own tests (§5.1): a .jl and a Markdown fixture found again after lines are inserted above them, after a binding is renamed, after the quote is reworded, and after both are gone (OUTDATED, never a guess).\n\nThe instruments whose findings come from agents are not measured: nothing plants known defects to count how many are found. This is a deliberate limit, not a plan: a change to an agent role's wording, rule list or strength cannot be shown to help or hurt, and whether they are worth their cost stays the owner's judgement, from what their reports contain. Every number in brackets is past evidence — from the science packages' review rounds and this package's own — kept as the reason a mechanism exists; nothing re-measures them from now on, so a role once justified by such a number stays justified only by it. Each run records what is factual and cheap: findings reported, fixes verified, items carried over, agent launches used and, where the platform reports it, tokens.","title":"12. What is shown to work, and what is not"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#13.-Change-control-and-growth","page":"Architecture","text":"A rule, agent role or instrument is code in GBGCodingTools, apart from the prose fields of §4.2: it changes through the code fixer on the owner's answer, is released as a tag that is never moved, and reaches a package through gbg bump [R2]; it never changes during a run. A design review is not an instrument and has no row in §3: gbg attack runs agents over this design and produces proposals, never findings, and each proposal becomes a question to the owner or is dismissed with its reason. It writes no tracked file — no run record, no report, no work-queue line — so it has no row in §6 either: its proposals reach the owner as questions in the session that ran it, the way a leak scan's findings do [as ruled].\n\nRetiring a rule. A rule leaves the rulebook by having its value removed, which is a code change like any other: the question proposing it names the id and why the rule is no longer worth keeping, the CHANGELOG entry records the removal, and the id is never reused [an id reused for a different rule makes every older record lie]. This is the counterpart of unchecked_reason, which says why a rule no program can check is kept: retirement is how one that should not be kept is dropped on the record rather than quietly [as ruled].\n\nGrowth limit. A code change adding a mechanism — a rule, agent role, instrument, run stage, verb or refusal — names, in its question, the requirement or failure it answers and what it replaces; without one it is not added.","title":"13. Change control and growth"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Witnesses/#Witnesses","page":"Witnesses","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Witnesses"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Witnesses/#GBGCodingTools.Witnesses","page":"Witnesses","text":"Witnesses\n\nThe witness runner of /bug-hunt. A witness is a self-contained Julia file asserting the CORRECT behaviour of the package, so it FAILS on a defective tree and PASSES once the defect is fixed; Witnesses.run executes every witness of a directory in a fresh process and holds the set to one expectation, reporting each file as a WitnessResult. The directory is usually a round's — a round being one run of one instrument (GBGCodingTools binds the term) over one consumer, recorded as a tracked directory under .gbg/rounds/. An agent's account of a program that fails is not that program; this is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Witnesses"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Witnesses/#GBGCodingTools.Witnesses.WitnessResult","page":"Witnesses","text":"WitnessResult\n\nThe outcome of one witness file.\n\nThe fields are:\n\nname — the file name.\nstatus — :pass, :fail (an assertion came out false — the witness working), :broken (an exception outside an assertion, a load error, or a pass with zero assertions — evidence of nothing), :blocked (the file declares # blocked: <reason>, reported its counts, errored and failed nothing; a run that reported no counts is :broken) or :refused (the file loads something it may not, or includes a file — never run).\npasses, fails, errors — the Test counts the run reported (zeros when it did not get that far).\nreason — the blocked reason, the refusal, or the first lines of a broken run's output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Witnesses.WitnessResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Witnesses/#GBGCodingTools.Witnesses.run","page":"Witnesses","text":"run(dir; expect, package, project, names = String[], io = stdout) -> (code, results)\n\nRuns every witness under dir and holds the set to an expectation. The arguments are:\n\ndir — the directory of .jl witnesses (the round's witnesses/, or test/regressions/escalated/).\nexpect — :fail (a hunt's bank: every witness must fail on this tree) or :pass (after the fix).\npackage — the package a witness may load.\nproject — the --project the witnesses run under.\nnames — restrict the run to the files whose names contain one of these.\nio — where the per-witness lines and the summary go.\n\nAn empty directory is a refusal (nothing was verified), exit 2, as is any REFUSED witness. A witness that PASSES when failure was expected is a PHANTOM (the defect does not exist) and one that FAILS when a pass was expected is UNFIXED; either is exit 1. A BROKEN witness is exit 1 too — evidence of nothing in either direction. BLOCKED witnesses are reported apart and do not fail the run.\n\nReturns the exit code and the per-witness results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Witnesses.run"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TestHarness/#TestHarness","page":"TestHarness","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"TestHarness"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TestHarness/#Allocation-and-traffic","page":"TestHarness","text":"","title":"Allocation and traffic"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TestHarness/#Parity,-oracles,-display,-threads","page":"TestHarness","text":"","title":"Parity, oracles, display, threads"},{"category":"section","location":"api/TestHarness/#Gates","page":"TestHarness","text":"","title":"Gates"},{"category":"module","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness","page":"TestHarness","text":"TestHarness\n\nThe engines behind a consumer's test/: the allocation audit (@alloc_below, TestHarness.allocation_completeness and the operations it covers, TestHarness.AUDITED_ALLOCATION_OPS), the element-traffic audit (TestHarness.counting, TestHarness.CountingArray, TestHarness.Traffic, TestHarness.audit, TestHarness.traffic_completeness and the operations it covers, TestHarness.AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS), the method-parity baselines (TestHarness.method_parity), the oracle registry (TestHarness.oracle_registry), the display gate (TestHarness.display_gate), the cross-thread-count digest (TestHarness.thread_digest), the promoted-witness loop (TestHarness.run_regressions). The frozen tables a package pins (method counts, traffic rows, oracle classes) live in the consumer as TOML; the engines here read them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.@alloc_below","page":"TestHarness","text":"@alloc_below bound expr\n\nWarms expr up once (compilation, plans, first touch), then asserts a single call allocates strictly fewer than bound bytes. A thrown error becomes a failed test rather than aborting the testset, so one bad line cannot mask every check after it. The callee of expr (when it is a plain call) is recorded in AUDITED_ALLOCATION_OPS. Splice a NAMED function rather than an anonymous closure: the expression is evaluated twice and an anonymous closure would get two types, the second read as a compile allocation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.@alloc_below"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.CountingArray","page":"TestHarness","text":"CountingArray{T,N} <: AbstractArray{T,N}\n\nA plain Array wrapper counting every getindex and setindex! with atomic counters, so an operation's element traffic can be measured through generic AbstractArray storage. A consumer using KernelAbstractions defines KernelAbstractions.get_backend(a::CountingArray) = get_backend(a.data) itself (the tool does not depend on it).\n\nThe fields are:\n\ndata — the wrapped array.\nreads — the atomic read counter.\nwrites — the atomic write counter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.CountingArray"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.counting","page":"TestHarness","text":"counting(data) -> CountingArray\n\nWraps data with zeroed counters. Returns the wrapper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.counting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.Traffic","page":"TestHarness","text":"Traffic\n\nOne array's row in an audited call.\n\nThe fields are:\n\nrole — the array's role in the call (\"source\", \"dest\", \"kernel\").\narray — the CountingArray measured.\nfrozen — the (reads, writes) asserted exactly.\nideal — the fused ideal documented beside it (each needed input element read once, each output written once).\nholder — the named holder REQUIRED when the two differ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.Traffic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.audit","page":"TestHarness","text":"audit(operation!, name, rows...; print = false)\n\nOne row of the traffic audit (the per-operation table of array reads and writes). The arguments are:\n\noperation! — a zero-argument closure running the operation once over the counting arrays.\nname — the row's name; its leading token, up to whitespace, ( or ∘, is the operation recorded in AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS.\nrows — one Traffic per array with its frozen (reads, writes).\nprint — print every measured count for re-baselining.\n\nIt zeroes the counters of every row, runs the operation, and asserts for every row that the measured pair equals the frozen pair exactly; a frozen count differing from its ideal must carry a named holder. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.audit"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.AUDITED_ALLOCATION_OPS","page":"TestHarness","text":"AUDITED_ALLOCATION_OPS\n\nThe operations @alloc_below has exercised in this process, recorded from the callee of each audited call so allocation_completeness can be DERIVED from the export list rather than trusted to memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.AUDITED_ALLOCATION_OPS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS","page":"TestHarness","text":"AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS\n\nThe operations audit has measured, taken from the leading token of each row name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.allocation_completeness","page":"TestHarness","text":"allocation_completeness(pkg, modules; exempt = Dict{Symbol,String}())\n\nThe derived completeness check of the allocation audit. The arguments are:\n\npkg — the package module.\nmodules — its owned modules (the public surface is derived from them).\nexempt — operation name to reason, for the operations deliberately not audited.\n\nEvery exported in-place operation (a name ending in ! on the ownership-derived public surface) was exercised by @alloc_below, or is named in exempt with a reason; no exemption names a vanished operation; every recorded callee is a real package function. Runs as a @testset. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.allocation_completeness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.traffic_completeness","page":"TestHarness","text":"traffic_completeness(pkg, modules; exempt = Dict{Symbol,String}())\n\nThe derived completeness check of the traffic audit, the twin of allocation_completeness over AUDITED_TRAFFIC_OPS. The arguments are:\n\npkg — the package module.\nmodules — its owned modules.\nexempt — operation name to reason, for the operations deliberately not audited.\n\nRuns as a @testset. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.traffic_completeness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.method_parity","page":"TestHarness","text":"method_parity(pkg, modules, baseline; externals = (Base,))\n\nThe method-parity engine. The arguments are:\n\npkg — the umbrella module.\nmodules — the package modules.\nbaseline — the parsed test/method_parity_baseline.toml.\nexternals — the external modules whose generics the package extends.\n\nEvery [[generic]] entry {set, name, count, module} asserts the number of methods the named module defines for that generic, each set's total is asserted where the baseline states [set_total], the [module] table asserts the derived per-module counts, and detect_ambiguities over pkg must be empty. A moved number is reported with the instruction to refreeze deliberately, with the reason. Runs as a @testset. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.method_parity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.oracle_registry","page":"TestHarness","text":"oracle_registry(pkg, modules, registry, testdir)\n\nThe oracle-provenance engine. The arguments are:\n\npkg — the umbrella module.\nmodules — the package modules.\nregistry — the parsed test/oracle_registry.toml ([[operation]] name, class, evidence).\ntestdir — the directory the cited test files live in.\n\nEvery public value-returning operation (the derived surface minus the ! names) is classified; no classification names a vanished operation; every class is one of analytic | independent | property | relational | structural; no operation rests on relational alone; and every non-structural citation names a test file under testdir that exists and mentions the operation. Runs as a @testset. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.oracle_registry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.display_gate","page":"TestHarness","text":"display_gate(modules; no_display = Dict{Symbol,String}, min_types = 1,\n             falsify = true)\n\nThe display gate, DERIVED rather than listed: every concrete struct type has a package-owned show or an exemption with a reason. The arguments are:\n\nmodules — the modules whose concrete struct types are enumerated.\nno_display — type name to reason, for the types deliberately without a display; an exemption whose type has a show after all is stale (a widened blind spot), and one naming a type no longer defined is stale.\nmin_types — the least number of types the enumeration must find, so a parser that stops matching fails rather than reporting a clean sweep.\nfalsify — with true, an empty parametric struct is planted in the first module and must be enumerated without a display.\n\nRuns as a @testset. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.display_gate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.thread_digest","page":"TestHarness","text":"thread_digest(probe_source; threads, project = Base.active_project()) -> String\n\nRuns a probe in a fresh Julia process with a given thread count and returns its output — the cross-thread-count half of a bitwise-invariance check the in-process toggle cannot make. The arguments are:\n\nprobe_source — Julia source that prints digests with fnv_digest (which is prepended).\nthreads — the -t value.\nproject — the --project it runs under.\n\nReturns the trimmed stdout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.thread_digest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/TestHarness/#GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.run_regressions","page":"TestHarness","text":"run_regressions(dir; parent = Main) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe one include loop of the promoted witnesses. The arguments are:\n\ndir — the consumer's test directory (@__DIR__ of runtests.jl); its regressions/ subdirectory is read, or dir itself when it is that directory.\nparent — the module the wrapper modules are evaluated in.\n\nEvery *.jl directly under the regressions directory (sorted) is included into its own wrapper module Regression_<name>, so top-level helpers cannot collide across files. An empty directory is an honest state and runs nothing. Returns the list of files run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.TestHarness.run_regressions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/config/#config","page":"The config","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\n.gbg/config.toml is the one config file of a consumer — a package that pins this tool and is reviewed by it: one layer, closed keys, validated by schema/config.schema.json through the tool's own validator. gbg init writes it from the package tree; gbg config --toml prints it with every default materialised; gbg schema config prints the schema.\n\nThe sections: [tool] (the pinned version), [package] (name, description, modules, the test and build commands), [paths], [git], [surfaces] (the ONE copy of every surface list: rationale documents, pages, generated copies, source, extensions, Literate examples, the built site, the history and references anchors, the glossary file, and any data or script surface whose comments are prose), [docs] (doctest, admonition titles, the Base allowance, retired claims), [rubric], [notation], [citations], [instruments] with one table per instrument, [cost], [schema], [[scans.custom]].\n\nSix values vary by package and are substituted into the rendered policy: rubric.leading_identity, rubric.signature_types (names or mirror — how much of the method a docstring signature repeats), notation.vectors, docs.admonition_titles, notation.mathbf_allowed (which never forbids \\mathbf{1}, the indicator and identity every package is granted) and instruments.check-docs.argcov_policy, the tier RULE.DOC.ARGCOV binds at. Everything else about the instruments is a constant of the tool, and changing one is a MAJOR release.","title":"The config"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.read","page":"The config","text":"read(root = find_root(); check = true) -> Dict\n\nLoads <root>/.gbg/config.toml, validates it against schema/config.schema.json, materialises every default and checks every path. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the consumer root, found by walking up from the working directory when not given.\ncheck — whether to run the path check (false for a config read from a tree that is not fully present).\n\nReturns the validated Dict with cfg[\"root\"] set; throws a ConfigError otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.read"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.parse","page":"The config","text":"Config.parse(text; root, check = true) -> Dict\n\nThe same validation, materialisation and path check as Config.read over the TOML text of a config instead of the file under root/.gbg/ — what the self-tests and the Step-0 scratch runs use to describe a tree without writing into it. The arguments are:\n\ntext — the config as TOML.\nroot — the tree it describes.\ncheck — whether to run the path check.\n\nReturns the validated Dict with cfg[\"root\"] = root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.parse"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.surface_files","page":"The config","text":"surface_files(cfg, key) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe files of one surface key of cfg[\"surfaces\"] — rationale, pages, src, ext, literate, data, or the compound keys docs (rationale + pages + literate) and all (docs + src + ext) — relative to the root, each file once, sorted. Generated pages (surfaces.generated.globs) are removed from pages, since they are build products. Returns the list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.surface_files"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.Config.resolve_anchor","page":"The config","text":"resolve_anchor(root, anchor) -> (file, line, count)\n\nResolves a file#heading ANCHOR — this sense of the word only, a Markdown reference to one heading. A hat's or lane's anchor is a different thing (whether it runs at full strength rather than on the economy model the module docstring defines, Preflight.resolve_hats), and a seed class's a third (Seeds.select). Here: file relative to root, heading a prefix of the text of exactly one Markdown heading (any # level) in it. Returns the file, the 1-based line of the match and the number of headings that matched — 0 or 2+ is a config error to the caller; the line is 0 when the file is absent or nothing matched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Config.resolve_anchor"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.validate","page":"The config","text":"validate(schema, value; root = schema, path = \"$\") -> Vector{String}\n\nValidates value against schema (a Dict in the JSON-Schema subset above). The arguments are:\n\nschema — the schema node to apply.\nvalue — the parsed document (TOML.parsefile or JSON3.read into a Dict).\nroot — the document the $refs resolve against; callers pass schema itself.\npath — the JSON path of value in the messages ($ at the top).\n\nReturns every violation as a message naming its path; an empty vector means valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.validate"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/config/#GBGCodingTools.load_schema","page":"The config","text":"load_schema(name) -> Dict\n\nReads schema/<name>.schema.json from the running copy into a Dict (JSON3, mutable copy). Returns the schema; a missing file is an ArgumentError naming it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.load_schema"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#rules","page":"The rules","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nGenerated at build time from this package's rendered policy copy under .gbg/policy/: the shared rulebook every consumer imports, with this package's parameters substituted. A rule's id is stable; the scans and the doctor cite it.","title":"The rules"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#The-docstring-rubric-(RULE.DOC.*,-RULE.API.*)","page":"The rules","text":"The organizing spec for every user-facing docstring and API page. Two audiences, one rule each: a module docstring is a map; a function docstring is a home.","title":"The docstring rubric (RULE.DOC.*, RULE.API.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.TWO_TIER","page":"The rules","text":"A module docstring is a map and a function docstring is a home. The map states what the module is, its scope and place in the architecture, and its invariants — then a linked one-liner per operation family, and no formulas; every formula lives in the operation's own docstring.","title":"RULE.DOC.TWO_TIER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.HOME_ORDER","page":"The rules","text":"A function docstring is ordered: identity, when-to-use, arguments, one formula, citation, links, caveats. In full: (1) a plain one-sentence identity in the user's terms; (2) when to use it, versus the alternatives; (3) each argument (positional and keyword) and its effect; (4) one formula if a clean one exists, never overflowing; (5) an inline citation for a named or published method and none for a classical one; (6) cross-links; (7) the caveats a user needs, as an admonition where load-bearing — the last block of the home's PROSE. Then the sections that are not prose, in this order and no other: the # References section of RULE.CIT.FULL_INLINE, then # Fields, which is last (RULE.DOC.SHAPE, and the shape scan reads any heading after # Fields as a hit). Three rules each used to claim the end of a docstring; the order lives here, and they defer to it. Excluded: implementation internals (a # comment), unexplained specialist jargon, architectural rationale and the formal apparatus (the rationale documents).","title":"RULE.DOC.HOME_ORDER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.LEADING_IDENTITY","page":"The rules","text":"Item (1) of the home is a plain one-sentence identity that leads with what the operation means, not how it is computed. For this package the identity leads with the physical meaning — what the operation does to its object, before how it is computed: an operation computed in a derived representation still states what it does to the real object first — with the explicit relation — and gives the derived form it uses after; an estimator states the population quantity it estimates, in standard probability notation, before the estimator. Where no clean form exists, say so briefly rather than forcing one.","title":"RULE.DOC.LEADING_IDENTITY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ONE_HOME","page":"The rules","text":"One concept has one home: one docstring per operation, on the bare binding, and a generic carries one docstring, never one per method. Per-scheme or per-representation specifics live in the scheme's or the representation's own docstring, linked. Across an f/f! pair the in-place ! form is the home — it owns the formula and the kernel and is listed first — and the allocating form and any operator alias is a thin wrapper that leads with the one-sentence meaning and @ref-links the home, carrying no second copy of the formula.","title":"RULE.DOC.ONE_HOME"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.VARIANT_PAIR","page":"The rules","text":"The review unit is the operation's whole variant set, so the defining math appears once across an f/f! or getter/setter pair and the wrapper carries no display formula. Typed twice, the math drifts — one member writing a partial one way and its twin another — and a within-string check cannot see it; a lane that groups each operation's generics and diffs them can.","title":"RULE.DOC.VARIANT_PAIR"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ARGS_FORM","page":"The rules","text":"A docstring documenting three or more arguments, or any keyword argument, uses the bulleted form led by The arguments are:; with fewer, the arguments are bound in running prose, and the two forms are never mixed in one docstring. The threshold counts what one signature takes, not the distinct names across type-specialised overloads. Exempt: a thin allocating wrapper, which documents no arguments at all; an internal _-prefixed helper, which documents its arguments in whatever form reads best; and a single element-type keyword bound in one prose clause.","title":"RULE.DOC.ARGS_FORM"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.FIELDS","page":"The rules","text":"A type that stores named, user-readable fields documents them under The fields are, one bullet per field, every bullet a real field and every field a bullet; prose is reserved for the one-sentence identity above the list. A type docstring is constructor-first: identity, the constructor and its arguments, an example where construction is non-obvious, then the demoted fields list.","title":"RULE.DOC.FIELDS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ADMONITION_TITLE","page":"The rules","text":"An admonition title is plain text stating the caveat, so a reader skimming the page learns the hazard without opening the block. For this package the rule is always: every !!! block carries a custom descriptive title: either every !!! block carries a custom descriptive title, or a block carrying one load-bearing claim does while a general aside may stay a bare !!! note. A caveat admonition is the last substantive block of a docstring, before the fields list.","title":"RULE.DOC.ADMONITION_TITLE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.STEER","page":"The rules","text":"Every ! form that has an allocating sibling carries the in-place steer sentence verbatim, after the identity paragraph and before the arguments, naming its own destination argument. The sentence is the template below — a template, not a link: destination is replaced by the argument the signature actually has and f by the allocating sibling's own name, so the @ref resolves in the docstring that carries it, never here.\n\nUse this in-place form when `destination` is already allocated; the allocating [`f`](@ref) returns a\nfresh result instead\n\nA ! form with no allocating sibling and a setter take no such sentence.","title":"RULE.DOC.STEER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ALIASING_BULLET","page":"The rules","text":"An operation-specific statement of whether the destination may alias an input lives in the destination argument's bullet, not as standalone prose. The package-wide guarantee lives in the module docstring and is not repeated per operation; an operation states it for itself only when it has an operation-specific reason, or when its contract is the contrary one.","title":"RULE.DOC.ALIASING_BULLET"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.INTERNAL_API","page":"The rules","text":"An ## Internal API section splits into ### Types and ### Functions only when it holds both kinds, comes last on its page, and opens with the same disclaimer sentence. A section documenting only functions uses a single unlabelled block and no ### at all.","title":"RULE.DOC.INTERNAL_API"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.REF_ABUT","page":"The rules","text":"A @ref never abuts a second code span, because two adjacent monospace chips render with a visible seam. Write the whole call as the link text with an explicit target: a Markdown link whose text is the backticked call and whose destination is @ref followed by the bare name.","title":"RULE.DOC.REF_ABUT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.API_GROUPING","page":"The rules","text":"API pages are math-grouped under hand-written headers via explicit @docs blocks, each object once, an f/f! pair together with the ! home first, no @index, and no empty block. A group is ordered by increasing complexity of its objects; a page leads with its headline operations, then the supporting types and schemes they take. checkdocs = :exports guards a dropped export, and every derived public operation has a home on a public page.","title":"RULE.DOC.API_GROUPING"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.COVERAGE_BASES","page":"The rules","text":"A reduction documents every base it supports, not just one. A measure reduction over a grid and the same reduction over a point set are both stated where the operation is documented.","title":"RULE.DOC.COVERAGE_BASES"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.GRAMMAR","page":"The rules","text":"Every sentence has a subject and a verb; no verbless fragments, noun stacks, label: colon tags or comma splices. Concise means few words, not missing verbs — compact prose that still reads as sentences aloud.","title":"RULE.DOC.GRAMMAR"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SENTENCE_OPENER","page":"The rules","text":"A sentence opens with a capitalized ordinary word, never a lowercase code identifier or a math formula. A lowercase start is reworded so an ordinary word leads; the identifier is never capitalized, its binding being lowercase by nature. Exempt: a bullet or list item, a # code comment (but not a Literate example's full-line prose comment, which renders), a heading, fenced code, and a capitalized type, module or file opener.","title":"RULE.DOC.SENTENCE_OPENER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.VERIFIED","page":"The rules","text":"Correctness is verified, not asserted: every formula, normalization, adjoint relation and cross-scheme equivalence is checked by expanding it against the code. A plausible \"X is just Y\" is a false claim until the expansion matches. Run this as a positive verification — enumerate every formula, constant, relation and quantifier and verify each — not as a read-through; a false-but-plausible statement is as invisible as an unbound symbol. Category and type errors are their own class: an object conflated with its representation, an operator with its parameter, a rate with its time-integral, a scalar with the field it reduces; type every object from the code.","title":"RULE.DOC.VERIFIED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SELF_CONTAINED","page":"The rules","text":"A rationale document reads top to bottom without another file: every piece of notation, a math symbol or a code identifier, is introduced in plain language at its first use. Later uses may be bare; universally standard mathematics may stand unglossed. A decision-log entry browsed on its own meets the same bar.","title":"RULE.DOC.SELF_CONTAINED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.TERMS_DEFINED","page":"The rules","text":"A term of art naming a package concept is defined in a clause or @ref-linked on its first use, and the acceptance test is the cold read. A docstring can bind every symbol and still be unreadable to someone who has not read the module, because its terms of art are nouns, not symbols; parse it with the module's context withheld, and a noun that silently assumes the package's context is the defect.","title":"RULE.DOC.TERMS_DEFINED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.CURRENT","page":"The rules","text":"Every claim matches the code's present state, and a summary that names only a subset of what the code now provides is stale even if every sentence is literally true. No stale future, deferred or none-exist claims; each module and operation summary still names everything added since it was written, against the current exports and method set. Retired phrasings a package bans are listed in its config and scanned.","title":"RULE.DOC.CURRENT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.CLASS_SWEEP","page":"The rules","text":"A flagged instance is a sample of a class: characterize the class, sweep every surface for it, and fix all occurrences in one pass, reporting the class and the count. Fixing only the pointed-at line and waiting to be handed the next instance is the failure this rule exists to stop. After a class fix, enumerate the surfaces from the finding's own text, not from memory, and re-measure.","title":"RULE.DOC.CLASS_SWEEP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.BATTERY","page":"The rules","text":"\"Check the docs\" means the complete battery over every surface, never one check or one file. When asked to check, review or audit the documentation in any words, run the full matrix — every check across every surface — and report it; do not run one check, sweep one file, and stop.","title":"RULE.DOC.BATTERY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.LITERATE","page":"The rules","text":"A Literate example is a rendered documentation page: every full-line # comment becomes prose, its math is LaTeX, and a comment that must stay in the code uses ##. A full-line comment inside a control-flow block splits the code fence, so reserve full-line # for narrative prose at statement boundaries. An underscored identifier in that prose is backticked, or Markdown parses it as an italic span.","title":"RULE.DOC.LITERATE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.BUILD_VERIFY","page":"The rules","text":"A documentation edit is verified by the full docs build exiting 0, never by a diff, a lint subset, or a grep of a log. A lint run standalone cannot see a dangling reference, and grepping a log for error text passes a build that died at load. After editing a Literate example, regenerate it and read the output.","title":"RULE.DOC.BUILD_VERIFY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.TABLE_TOP_LEVEL","page":"The rules","text":"A Markdown table is top-level, never indented inside a list item, and no cell holds a literal |. The renderer will not form a table inside an indented item and falls back to literal pipes; inside a list use a nested bullet list, and write a norm bar as LaTeX, never as a bare pipe.","title":"RULE.DOC.TABLE_TOP_LEVEL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ONE_NOTATION","page":"The rules","text":"One notation per concept and one concept per notation; a local use yields to a package-wide glyph. Before introducing a symbol, check it is not a synonym for one already in use, and check the section for the glyph and confirm it is free. When an edit introduces a symbol, retire the old one.","title":"RULE.DOC.ONE_NOTATION"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.COORD_FREE","page":"The rules","text":"Maps and pairings are stated intrinsically, and coordinate or matrix machinery that buries the structure is confined to the formalization document. Component notation for a genuine fiber algebra is fine; the rule targets flattening a structured space to a bare vector. Where a basis form is needed to mirror the code, state the basis and the space it acts on.","title":"RULE.DOC.COORD_FREE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.LINKS","page":"The rules","text":"Cross-referencing is discriminating-minimalist: backtick every code identifier and promote to @ref only where a reader would genuinely jump and there is one clear destination. Link a target at most once per docstring; never link the docstring's own binding, its arguments, option tokens, struct fields or Base names; a family of built-ins is one catalogue destination unless the members are distinct types a reader must choose between; sibling names in one list are all linked or all plain, never a mix. The at-home test: if a sentence would sit equally well in the target's own docstring, cut it and link, the function's own defining formula and validity clauses always staying.","title":"RULE.DOC.LINKS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SIBLING_LINK","page":"The rules","text":"The first mention of a sibling GBG package in a page, a rationale document, a Literate example or a docstring is a link to that package's documentation site, written [Package](https://<owner>.github.io/Package.jl/). A later mention in the same unit does not link that site again — it is a bare code span, or a deep link to a named page of the site, which is a cross-reference rather than the repetition this rule is about. A package never links its own name. The names and sites come from the fleet registry the tool ships (fleet.toml), so the target is one string in every package.","title":"RULE.DOC.SIBLING_LINK"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.NO_DEAD_REFS","page":"The rules","text":"A rendered docstring or a thrown error message cites no section number, repo path or inaccessible source, and reads complete at the REPL where links are dead. A cross-reference adds depth one hop away; it never carries a fact the reader needs. Section citations and design rationale stay in # comments and the rationale documents.","title":"RULE.DOC.NO_DEAD_REFS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.AI_BANNER","page":"The rules","text":"Every AI-written rationale page and Literate example carries the one provenance banner verbatim, and the banner is identical wherever it appears. The banner is the two lines !!! info \"Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\" and This page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.; a Literate file carries them as # comment lines, and a drifted copy is a finding. The site's home page (docs/src/index.md) never carries the banner in any package: the owner reviews that one page in every package, and a banner there would be false. A package that wants the banner on more of its pages says so in its own CLAUDE.md.","title":"RULE.DOC.AI_BANNER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SHAPE","page":"The rules","text":"A docstring's longest paragraph stays within 120 words and it carries at most three admonitions, no unit grows past its recorded baseline, an # Arguments heading — where a consumer uses one in place of the bulleted form of RULE.DOC.ARGS_FORM — precedes any column-0 admonition, and # Fields is last. The caps bind on every docstring and every page unit that is new; a page unit already in the baseline is held by the ratchet alone, which is the one place the baseline is the whole of the check. A paragraph is a run of lines between blank ones, so a Markdown table is ONE paragraph however long: that is why the caps bind on docstrings, where a table is rare, and why an inherited page section is held by the ratchet. A docstring that has taken three or more rounds of patches is rewritten whole rather than appended to again; the shape baseline is re-recorded deliberately, never by accident.","title":"RULE.DOC.SHAPE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.MODULE_MAP","page":"The rules","text":"A module docstring lists one linked one-liner per operation family and carries no formula. Its order is identity, then the contents list, then an optional trailing remark.","title":"RULE.DOC.MODULE_MAP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ARG_BULLET","page":"The rules","text":"An argument or field bullet names its argument in a plain code span, never a bold one. The form is a list item holding the backticked name, an em dash and what it is, one bullet per argument.","title":"RULE.DOC.ARG_BULLET"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SIGNATURE_MIRROR","page":"The rules","text":"A docstring signature names the method's arguments in the method's order, and only the docstring wraps, to at most 88 columns with a hanging indent under the first argument; the source definition is left exactly as written. How much of the method the signature repeats is this package's to set, and it is the names and their defaults, and no types: a type belongs to the argument's own bullet under The arguments are:, where there is room to say what it constrains, and repeating it in the signature line costs a second place to keep in step. Whichever form a package takes, the two never drift: the signature is checked against the method it documents, not against a reader's memory of it.","title":"RULE.DOC.SIGNATURE_MIRROR"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.SIGNATURE_FIDELITY","page":"The rules","text":"The displayed signature set and the defined method table agree per exported binding, and the symmetric difference is reported. A displayed keyword no method has is a stale signature; a defined keyword no signature shows is unadvertised. A triage scan: an inner constructor deliberately omitted, an extended external generic and a kwargs... passthrough produce legitimate asymmetries.","title":"RULE.DOC.SIGNATURE_FIDELITY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.XLINK","page":"The rules","text":"A cross-module name is @ref-linked at its first mention in a docstring, qualified so the link resolves. An unqualified cross-module @ref does not resolve, and the author who downgrades it to a bare span creates a link that is silently missing rather than broken.","title":"RULE.DOC.XLINK"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.ARGCOV","page":"The rules","text":"Every argument in a docstring signature, positional and keyword, is documented in its body, and the sweep runs from the signature. An argument that surfaces only inside a formula reads as bound by use and slips past a symbol sweep; the accepted forms are a plain span and a span carrying the default. For this package the check is a gate tier.","title":"RULE.DOC.ARGCOV"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.REGISTER","page":"The rules","text":"A tunable is an option, a setting or a keyword argument — never a \"knob\" or a \"tweak\". The reference register is neutral across every authored surface: docstrings, rationale documents, doc pages, # comments and testset names.","title":"RULE.DOC.REGISTER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.DOC.RETURNS","page":"The rules","text":"A type docstring leads with its constructor, and a -> Type arrow or a Returns x. sentence states the result where the identity sentence does not. A mutator whose signature carries no arrow says what it returns in a sentence before any caveat; a signature carries an arrow only where the return type is fixed by the signature; arrow and sentence never both appear.","title":"RULE.DOC.RETURNS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.API.POSITIONAL","page":"The rules","text":"A result-affecting argument is positional, a performance hint or a builder's cost and accuracy tunable is a keyword, and every exception is recorded. In an operation, every argument that affects the result is positional, defaults included; an argument that cannot change the result is a keyword. An exception is a ledger deviation with its reason.","title":"RULE.API.POSITIONAL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#The-doc-edit-checklist","page":"The rules","text":"Run all of these on every documentation change, most strictly on a rationale document, each as a positive procedure: enumerate, then verify each item. (1) Correct — RULE.DOC.VERIFIED. (2) Self-contained — RULE.DOC.SELF_CONTAINED, RULE.DOC.TERMS_DEFINED, RULE.DOC.ARGCOV. (3) Notation — RULE.DOC.ONE_NOTATION and latex-idiom.md. (4) Formatted, bidirectional — math is LaTeX never backticks, every code identifier backticked never bare, and the silent-rendering rules. (5) Current and complete — RULE.DOC.CURRENT. (6) Cited — citations.md. (7) Across variant pairs — RULE.DOC.VARIANT_PAIR. Order the work code before docs: the docs describe the code, so the reverse order writes every affected docstring twice.","title":"The doc-edit checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#The-LaTeX-idiom-(RULE.TEX.*)","page":"The rules","text":"Every rule here is standard LaTeX idiom plus a few documented exceptions, not a matter of taste; when unsure, reach for a standard LaTeX reference and KaTeX's published support table, never a plausible-sounding typographic argument. Documenter renders KaTeX client-side, so a formula's TeX is the page's text.","title":"The LaTeX idiom (RULE.TEX.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.RENDERED_MATH","page":"The rules","text":"All mathematics in docstrings and doc pages is rendered LaTeX, never a Unicode-glyph approximation, and every code object is bound to its math symbol on first mention. Inline math is a double-backtick span, display math a fenced block whose language is math; inside a \"\"\" docstring every LaTeX backslash is doubled. Plain # comments stay Unicode, being unrendered. Write the binding explicitly — laplacian equiv Delta — so the reader always knows which symbol is which function, and write it as one span inside a rendered docstring.","title":"RULE.TEX.RENDERED_MATH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.CODE_IN_MATH","page":"The rules","text":"A code identifier never sits inside a math span, because the renderer sets it as the product of italic single letters. Backtick it in prose; a code name that must appear inside a formula is \\texttt{…}.","title":"RULE.TEX.CODE_IN_MATH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.ONE_SPAN","page":"The rules","text":"A formula is one LaTeX span, never split across a math span and an adjacent code span. Keep it one span, a trailing code name in \\texttt{}.","title":"RULE.TEX.ONE_SPAN"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.INLINE_ONE_LINE","page":"The rules","text":"An inline math span stays on one source line, because a wrapped continuation that begins with a list or blockquote leader is parsed as a new list item and silently drops the span out of math mode. Break the prose around the span, never the span; the docs build fails on the defect.","title":"RULE.TEX.INLINE_ONE_LINE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.NO_HSCROLL","page":"The rules","text":"Nothing scrolls horizontally: a long docstring signature is hand-wrapped with a hanging indent under the first argument, and a wide display formula is broken with aligned, gathered or split. Neither a rendered code block nor display math auto-wraps; inline math reflows and is exempt. Never pack several equations on one line behind \\quad.","title":"RULE.TEX.NO_HSCROLL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.CODE_SPAN_JULIA","page":"The rules","text":"A backtick span holds only well-formed Julia — a name, type, call or expression — never math that merely looks code-ish. A half-open interval, an inequality, a product, a lone ≤ or · glyph is LaTeX; the real operators ×, ⋅ and ≈ backticked are code and stay.","title":"RULE.TEX.CODE_SPAN_JULIA"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.SPACING","page":"The rules","text":"Never space manually around a relation or binary operator, never put \\! before a \\bigl, never \\!\\! before a sum, and never write \\, except before an upright differential. Spacing next to =, <, \\in, + or - doubles the space TeX already puts there; \\! before a delimiter that follows an operator or name jams the delimiter into it, there being no gap to remove; between multiplied factors juxtaposition is standard, and when a gratuitous thin space is removed it becomes a plain space, never nothing. Correct and not to be \"fixed\": \\sim\\!, \\,\\mathrm{d}x, \\quad/\\qquad between display equations, and \\mathrm{Re} versus \\operatorname{Re} before a delimiter.","title":"RULE.TEX.SPACING"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.MATHBB","page":"The rules","text":"\\mathbb is written only on a capital A–Z or k, and the indicator or identity is \\mathbf{1}. KaTeX's blackboard font covers only those glyphs, so \\mathbb on a digit falls back to a plain glyph with the font silently dropped.","title":"RULE.TEX.MATHBB"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.MATHBF","page":"The rules","text":"\\mathbf is admitted only for an agreed list of strings, and vectors and tensors follow the package's one vector notation. For this package the admitted strings are none and the vector notation is not used (the package has no vector quantities): component notation with index letters, or bold italic \\boldsymbol{u} with a braced argument, or no vector notation at all. Never the other, and never an upright \\mathbf outside the admitted list. \\mathbf{1}, the indicator and identity of RULE.TEX.MATHBB, is admitted in every package whatever that list holds; it is not a package's to withhold.","title":"RULE.TEX.MATHBF"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.LEFTRIGHT","page":"The rules","text":"\\left and \\right are banned; every delimiter is sized by hand with \\bigl/\\Bigl … \\bigr/\\Bigr. Manual delimiters are Open and Close atoms with no added gap, where \\left … \\right is an Inner atom that adds a spurious gap around the fence. Sole exception: a system or cases written \\left\\{ … \\right.; the arrow commands are not delimiters and are unaffected.","title":"RULE.TEX.LEFTRIGHT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.PARTIAL","page":"The rules","text":"A partial derivative is \\partial_{x_i}, subscripting the coordinate, and a bare-index \\partial_i is banned. A second-order derivative is \\partial^2_{x_i x_i}, a repeated index summed.","title":"RULE.TEX.PARTIAL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.IMAGINARY","page":"The rules","text":"The imaginary unit is upright \\mathrm{i}, and i, j, k are indices. Never a bare italic i, never \\imath or \\mathbb{i}; the differential is likewise upright, \\,\\mathrm{d}x.","title":"RULE.TEX.IMAGINARY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.FRAC_BRACED","page":"The rules","text":"Every fraction argument is braced: \\tfrac{1}{2}, never \\tfrac12 and never \\tfrac N2. Both render alike; the braces are the house style and the scan is exact.","title":"RULE.TEX.FRAC_BRACED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.BOLDSYMBOL_BRACED","page":"The rules","text":"Every \\boldsymbol argument is braced: \\boldsymbol{u}, never \\boldsymbol u. The unbraced form takes one token and silently stops at a subscript.","title":"RULE.TEX.BOLDSYMBOL_BRACED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.SWALLOWED_ESCAPE","page":"The rules","text":"Inside a Julia docstring every LaTeX backslash is doubled, because a single backslash before a, b, e, f, n, r, t, v, an octal digit, x, u or U is swallowed by the string parser. The rendered formula then lacks the command with no error anywhere.","title":"RULE.TEX.SWALLOWED_ESCAPE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.RENDERED_MARKUP","page":"The rules","text":"No emphasis marker survives into rendered prose. An emphasis nesting CommonMark cannot resolve, such as *a **b***, renders as literal asterisks; the built page, not the source, is where it shows, and the docs build scans the built HTML for it.","title":"RULE.TEX.RENDERED_MARKUP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEX.GREEK_AB","page":"The rules","text":"Accumulation scalars are written Unicode α and β everywhere — arguments, locals, kernel bodies and docstrings — and the named ASCII holdouts stay. A hand-written operation spelling them alpha/beta is drift; a flag naming the α/β variant and an external window-shape beta in an English-register block are the recorded exceptions.","title":"RULE.TEX.GREEK_AB"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Citations-(RULE.CIT.*)","page":"The rules","text":"","title":"Citations (RULE.CIT.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.CIT.STYLE","page":"The rules","text":"A citation is context-standard author–year: author-prominent with a parenthesized year, a bare year inside an existing parenthetical, at most three authors named, and First et al. for four or more. Keep enough surnames to disambiguate two works that would otherwise collide. An eponymous method name takes no year unless the work is actually cited.","title":"RULE.CIT.STYLE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.CIT.FULL_INLINE","page":"The rules","text":"A docstring cites author–year in its prose and carries the full reference in a # References section after the home's prose, before any # Fields: one bullet per work, Authors, [*Title*](https://doi.org/…), Journal Volume (Year), verbatim-identical to the canonical entry under the package's references heading, title and DOI compared. Never the full reference in the running prose, which the sentence has to carry and the reader has to step over. Doc pages cite short and the canonical full reference lives in the rationale document; a REPL reader sees the whole reference without opening another file, and drift between the copies is the chosen duplication's only risk, so it is the thing checked.","title":"RULE.CIT.FULL_INLINE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.CIT.DOI","page":"The rules","text":"Every reference carries a clickable DOI on its title, [*Title*](https://doi.org/…), or an Open Library link for a book with no DOI, identical in every copy. A DOI stays REPL-safe; a link target percent-encodes () and <>, and the scan compares DOIs decoded.","title":"RULE.CIT.DOI"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.CIT.BLOCK_LIST","page":"The rules","text":"Two or more citations form a bullet list under a References: label, one citation per line, and a lone citation stays inline as Reference: …. A semicolon-joined paragraph is unreadable when rendered.","title":"RULE.CIT.BLOCK_LIST"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.CIT.NO_INACCESSIBLE","page":"The rules","text":"Never cite an inaccessible source on any user-facing surface: not a gitignored monograph, an accompanying thesis, an appendix or figure the reader cannot open, nor working notes. A citation the reader cannot follow is dead weight and implies they are missing something they cannot get; present every result on its own public terms.","title":"RULE.CIT.NO_INACCESSIBLE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#The-testing-protocol-(RULE.TEST.*)","page":"The rules","text":"","title":"The testing protocol (RULE.TEST.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.GATE_VS_SURVEY","page":"The rules","text":"Pkg.test() is a gate and the exhaustive suite is a survey; a specific oracle and every completeness guard go in the gate. A gate re-checks claims already known and must be green or the change does not ship; a survey searches for combinations nobody enumerated and is read, not passed. A universal oracle over a product goes in the survey, and anything that may legitimately be non-green goes there too, because a suppressed failure reads as a pass.","title":"RULE.TEST.GATE_VS_SURVEY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.ORACLE_INDEPENDENT","page":"The rules","text":"Every public value-returning operation is classified in the oracle registry, and relational-only coverage is a finding. The classes are analytic (a closed form derived independently of the package), independent (a direct computation written in the test), property (an invariant that pins the operation without computing it), relational (compared only against other package operations — legitimate as a second layer, never as the only one) and structural (returns metadata or storage). A new operation fails the registry until someone classifies it, which is the moment to notice it has no independent oracle.","title":"RULE.TEST.ORACLE_INDEPENDENT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.DEFINITION_OF_DONE","page":"The rules","text":"A feature is done when its correctness tests, its performance test, the guiding documents, the remaining documents, the benchmarks built and run, and the examples run are each verified, not assumed. \"Should work\" is not done: a step is done when its verification command exits 0 and the exit code was observed. Grep the docs for stale deferred, none-exist and future claims about whatever was just implemented.","title":"RULE.TEST.DEFINITION_OF_DONE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.ALLOC_ZERO","page":"The rules","text":"Every in-place ! operator is allocation-free and appears in the allocation audit, a deliberate materializer gets a bounded guard, and where a bound covers another component's nondeterminism a configuration pins the true zero. The audit runs on the CPU, where a materialized temporary is visible; the completeness of its rows is derived from the export list, never remembered.","title":"RULE.TEST.ALLOC_ZERO"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.PASS_AUDIT","page":"The rules","text":"Every audited operation has a frozen per-array traffic row, the fused ideal documented beside it, and a named holder for any gap. A raised count is a fusion regression unless a new holder justifies it; the frozen values are refrozen in the same commit as the change, with the reason.","title":"RULE.TEST.PASS_AUDIT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.METHOD_PARITY","page":"The rules","text":"The public method set is frozen per generic and per module, and any change moves a number that is then refrozen deliberately, with the reason. The per-module count is derived from ownership over the whole function surface, internals included, so a method defined under a qualified name cannot hide.","title":"RULE.TEST.METHOD_PARITY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.MUTATION","page":"The rules","text":"A symmetric fixture cannot see an asymmetric defect, a self-referential assertion cannot see a shared defect, and a stated convention must be falsifiable by its fixture. Inject the defect and require the suite to catch it; a fixture that cannot reach the broken branch proves nothing about it.","title":"RULE.TEST.MUTATION"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.BOTH_DIRECTIONS","page":"The rules","text":"Test the check in both directions: a check that cannot fail is not a check, and a self-reported field is acceptable only when the missing case is the failing case. The flattering direction is the one nobody guards: a check that demotes sound answers is stopped being read, and a check nobody reads passes everything.","title":"RULE.TEST.BOTH_DIRECTIONS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.MISSING_IS_FAILING","page":"The rules","text":"The missing case is the failing case: a denominator is derived, an absence arm exits non-zero, and a zero count is a dead lane rather than a clean one. An empty witness directory, an empty ledger, an unparsable status — each fails; \"nothing to check\" is not \"all clear\".","title":"RULE.TEST.MISSING_IS_FAILING"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.RULE_WITH_CHECK","page":"The rules","text":"A rule and its check land in the same commit, and a stated rule without a check is a known weakness. A rule is written by whoever has just fixed an instance, at the moment they are most confident the class is handled — which is precisely when nobody builds the check; afterwards the rule's presence reads as compliance. When a rule is written down, name what would catch its next violation in its RULES.toml check field: a scan:, doctor: or test: id, a harness: binding the tool enforces it by, panel for a rule a reading panel decides inside an instrument that runs, or weakness — a check is possible and none is built. Only panel is an answer without a program behind it, and only because the panel is itself a procedure with a roster, a record and a verdict; \"someone remembering this rule\" is not one, and a rule resting on nothing but that is weakness however old the habit. A rule no program could ever decide is panel; weakness is reserved for the check that could exist and does not. A sweep reporting zero is the one to distrust: falsify it against a fixture carrying a known positive before believing it.","title":"RULE.TEST.RULE_WITH_CHECK"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.EXAMPLES_RUN","page":"The rules","text":"Shipped examples are executed after any change to the public surface, because nothing else runs them. An example is both a rendered page and a downloadable script; the docs build renders it without running it, and a call that no longer dispatches is found only by running it.","title":"RULE.TEST.EXAMPLES_RUN"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.TEST.COUNTS_REMEASURED","page":"The rules","text":"A count nothing asserts is re-measured, never cited, and a drop against the last recorded figure is a finding. A test count, a testset count, a coverage figure — each is measured at the time it is stated.","title":"RULE.TEST.COUNTS_REMEASURED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#The-instruments-(RULE.INST.*)","page":"The rules","text":"warning: Being rebuilt\nThe instruments these rules govern are being rebuilt to docs/ARCHITECTURE.md of GBGCodingTools, adopted 2026-09-17; the machinery that ran them was deleted that day and none of them runs today. The rules below are kept because they are the rulebook every package pins, and because the rebuilt instruments must still satisfy them or say why not — but ten of them name a check that no longer exists and are marked weakness in the register until the rebuild gives them one.","title":"The instruments (RULE.INST.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.CONSENT","page":"The rules","text":"Unasked, run only Pkg.test(); every instrument, survey, benchmark and panel runs only on the user's request, in words or by slash. The correct output when one would be informative is to name it and its cost and wait. This is a consent boundary, not licence to leave work unfinished: finish the code, run the gate, and report what remains unverified and why. \"Check the docs\" is such a request: it means run /check-docs.","title":"RULE.INST.CONSENT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.FOUR_OBJECTS","page":"The rules","text":"The four instruments audit four different objects: /audit-changes the changes to code, /check-docs the source prose, /check-consistency the rendered prose, /bug-hunt the code. A flawless docstring on a function returning the wrong number passes both prose instruments; an edit that is a correct fix and still wider than the defect passes the hunt; each exists for what the others cannot see.","title":"RULE.INST.FOUR_OBJECTS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.WITNESS","page":"The rules","text":"A static claim about code is a hypothesis; the only admissible evidence is a program that fails; a fixed witness is promoted unchanged. A passing witness means not a bug, discarded however good the argument; no witness writable means a latent hazard, reported and not fixed; a failing witness is the finding and, once the fix lands, the regression test — necessary and not sufficient, since a witness can fail because the witness is wrong, so adjudication derives the expected value independently of the assertion.","title":"RULE.INST.WITNESS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.SELF_REPORT","page":"The rules","text":"Self-reported closure is not closure: the act verifier sees the diff, the state verifier is blind, and the demotion is mechanical. Both default to the unfavourable verdict so a truncated lane fails safe; a zero count is a dead lane rather than a clean hat; the denominator never comes from the party being measured; and a dismissal is a verdict the verifier grades like any other.","title":"RULE.INST.SELF_REPORT"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.CLOSURE_LOOP","page":"The rules","text":"The closure loop runs over the banked findings by id, never by re-running the battery, and abandon is the only wholesale exit and names every open id. Closing a banked round terminates because the denominator is fixed; re-running the lanes over a just-edited corpus is a new search with no fixed point. Stop on a plateau — three passes flat within noise, with no dead lane — never on a good round.","title":"RULE.INST.CLOSURE_LOOP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.CARRY","page":"The rules","text":"A round may close by CARRYING a finding into the next round of its instrument: a minor or a nit whenever the owner chooses, and every finding still open once the round has spent its pass budget, a blocker or a major among them. The budget is max_passes verification passes, 3 by default: every pass re-runs both verifiers, and past the third 2026-09-10-check-docs-03 spent six more re-verifying fixes already made. Past the budget severity decides releasability, not closability: a final release refuses while a carried blocker or major stands. The severity that counts is a re-rating by someone other than the lane that reported it, because a rating that can defer a finding is a rating its author has a reason to lower. A carried finding is re-banked into the next round after its lanes have run — never shown to the searching panel, and never left to be rediscovered, since a fresh battery is a fresh search and a finding trusted to luck is a finding dropped. Across rounds the constraint is that the pile drains, which a final release checks, and no finding rides more than three rounds before the owner rules on it instead.","title":"RULE.INST.CARRY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.AUDIT_AFTER_CODE","page":"The rules","text":"A fix pass that changed code is audited before its round closes. /bug-hunt always qualifies, and so does a /check-docs round whose pass touched the src or ext surfaces: closing refuses until a /audit-changes round based at the closing round's opening head is itself closed and has read the round's last code change, so it runs after the final fix pass. The two verifiers grade each finding as asked; neither reads the diff whole, runs anything, or asks whether input that worked before still does. A prose-only pass is not gated: run the audit's prose lenses on judgment when the pass rewrote docstrings that state a contract.","title":"RULE.INST.AUDIT_AFTER_CODE"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.AUDIT_DISPOSED","page":"The rules","text":"An audit round is closed by disposing its concerns, never by correcting them inside it. A correction moves the tree, the staleness check then refuses the close, and there is no re-bank; the round would be permanently unclosable. Each gated concern takes a disposition record instead, and a correction the audit asks for rides the next change set, which the next audit covers. This is why bank on an audit round names the disposition and not a fix pass.","title":"RULE.INST.AUDIT_DISPOSED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.FIX_FROM_LANES","page":"The rules","text":"A fix pass is driven from the per-lane findings by id, never from the consolidated summary. Consolidation is lossy by design, which is what makes it readable; a severity in the summary never overrides the lane's own, and closure is reported per lane and per id.","title":"RULE.INST.FIX_FROM_LANES"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.SEEDS","page":"The rules","text":"A seeded finding is never fixed in the corpus and never enters the ledger. Seeds are planted in a disposable copy to measure recall; the ground truth reaches no hat; a class at zero seeded recall is unmeasured, not clean; and recording a seed as adjudicated would hand the next round its answer key.","title":"RULE.INST.SEEDS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.NO_TRUNCATION","page":"The rules","text":"A scan's output is never truncated by head, tail or a limit: count first, then read all. Adding a limit to keep the output readable is how a class gets half-swept and reported closed; a deterministic check that depends on who types it is not deterministic.","title":"RULE.INST.NO_TRUNCATION"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.FROZEN_MIDROUND","page":"The rules","text":"An instrument is never changed mid-round; a change is its own commit and release, and reaches a consumer by a bump. A change the round wanted and could not make is written one line per deferral in the round's deferred.md, and gbg round close carries each into .claude/WORK_QUEUE.md, where the Stop hook holds the next session to it. A deferral recorded anywhere else is a note, and a note is not a mechanism. A round records the instrument version that measured it and cannot close under a different one unless re-graded; a rate sequence means something only at a fixed instrument. One relaxation is recorded rather than hidden: a round whose own frozen verifier or auditor scripts cannot run — its verifiers unparseable, so it could never verify — has those differing copies replaced by gbg round scripts --reason, which writes the reason, the pass count and each script's sha into ROUND.toml. A lens script is never replaced mid-round: a changed lens is a new round.","title":"RULE.INST.FROZEN_MIDROUND"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.INST.ECONOMY","page":"The rules","text":"Economy lanes are the default, the five phases — adjudication, reproduction, verification, consolidation, the fix pass — are never economised, and full strength runs on explicit request. A cheap lane's false positive is caught downstream; a false negative in a lane with no downstream verifier is why some lanes are anchors and stay at full strength in every mode.","title":"RULE.INST.ECONOMY"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Honest-limits","page":"The rules","text":"Stated, not claimed closed: grader/graded separation holds for code and prompts, not for files a session can forge — the residue is transcript-visible, not gate-visible. A consistency round's seeded copy lives inside the project, gitignored, and a docs round's in a temporary directory outside it that bank deletes; a hat that opens it, or diffs its manuscript against the built site, leaves a read in its transcript and nothing in the tally except, sometimes, a suspect flag on the hats that did not. A GBG_ROUND exported by hand outside a CLI invocation makes the gate ignore that round exactly as a CLI-run build would; the export is in the transcript, and CI never sets it. A working tree's gate can see an open round that was not committed; CI proves only the pushed state.","title":"Honest limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Git-conventions-(RULE.GIT.*)","page":"The rules","text":"","title":"Git conventions (RULE.GIT.*)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.PUSH","page":"The rules","text":"Commit ⇒ push, directly on the configured branch; no feature branches. The repository is private and synced across machines through GitHub, so work is committed on the default branch and pushed in the same step; GitHub is the cross-machine sync path and the docs site deploys on push. Stage with git add -u plus every new file by name, never -A.","title":"RULE.GIT.PUSH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.TRAILER","page":"The rules","text":"Every commit message ends with the versionless trailer Co-Authored-By: Anthropic Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>, and never carries [skip ci]. The trailer names no model version on purpose: the model in use changes often and the versions supersede each other, so a version in the trailer is a fact about the day rather than about the work, and it goes stale in the one place git never lets anyone correct it.","title":"RULE.GIT.TRAILER"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.CI_MANUAL","page":"The rules","text":"CI is manual-only; the matrix runs locally before a commit, and the docs deploy on every push. Before committing a code change, run the gate locally; a documentation-only change needs a load and the docs build. The docs build is therefore the per-push enforcement point, and the doctor, the hard scans and the gate stay inside it.","title":"RULE.GIT.CI_MANUAL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.NO_SHIMS","page":"The rules","text":"The API is not frozen; no backward-compatibility shims are built. Errors found later are fixed one by one; architectural soundness is judged on the full picture first.","title":"RULE.GIT.NO_SHIMS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.BRANCH","page":"The rules","text":"The working branch is the one the package's config names, master by default. A rename is a decision about the package, never bundled with a tooling adoption.","title":"RULE.GIT.BRANCH"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.MANIFEST_TRACKED","page":"The rules","text":"Sub-environment manifests are gitignored, and a tracked root manifest is a declared deviation. A stale sub-environment manifest is resolved locally when precompilation breaks; CI resolves its own.","title":"RULE.GIT.MANIFEST_TRACKED"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#RULE.GIT.REQUESTS","page":"The rules","text":"A cross-package request is a tracked file .gbg/requests/to-<PKG>-<date>-<topic>.md in the requester with a status paragraph on top, and a concluded request is deleted, not kept. The requests directory holds what is still awaiting the other package and nothing else.","title":"RULE.GIT.REQUESTS"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#glossary","page":"The rules","text":"Consumer — a package that pins the tool: the four GBG packages, the tool repository itself, and the toy fixture its tests run over.\nInstrument — one of the four review procedures: /check-docs, /check-consistency, /bug-hunt, /audit-changes. The word keeps its house meaning of a measuring device.\nRound — one run of an instrument, recorded as a tracked directory under .gbg/rounds/.\nLane / hat — one agent of an instrument: a hat is a persona in the panel, a lane a surface or territory in the other three.\nAnchor — a hat or lane that runs at full strength instead of the economy model.\nGate — a program whose non-zero exit blocks a build, a test run or a round's closure.\nPinned version — the exact tool version a consumer names in .gbg/config.toml; the doctor's PIN.* checks compare it with what the sub-environments and the running copy resolve.\nManaged file — a file the tool renders into a consumer and hash-checks thereafter; edited only by gbg sync.\nLedger — the consumer's one file of declared exceptions, .gbg/exemptions.toml. The bug-hunt's per-finding fixed | refused | escalated record is the disposition record, never \"the ledger\".\nWitness — a self-contained test asserting the correct behaviour of the package, observed to fail on a defective tree; the only admissible evidence of a code defect.\nHome / map — a function docstring is a home (it owns the operation's formula); a module docstring is a map (linked one-liners, no formulas).\nCold read — parsing a docstring with the module's source and the design documents withheld; the acceptance test for self-containedness.\nClass sweep — treating a flagged instance as a sample and fixing every occurrence on every surface.\nEstimand / estimator — the population quantity an operation estimates, and the finite-sample rule that approximates it; where the package's leading identity is the estimand, the docstring states the first before the second.\nRepresentation — how an object is stored or computed (physical, spectral); an object is never named by its representation — \"a field in spectral representation\", never \"a spectral field\".\nThe house notation set — upright \\mathrm{i} and \\mathrm{d}, coordinate-subscripted \\partial_{x_i}, braced fraction and \\boldsymbol arguments, no gratuitous \\,, no manual spacing around relations, no \\left/\\right, \\mathbb on capitals and k only; the vector notation and the admitted \\mathbf strings are the package's own parameters.","title":"Glossary — the terms the rules use"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Declared-deviations-—-GBGCodingTools","page":"The rules","text":"Rendered by gbg sync from .gbg/exemptions.toml: every live entry of scope policy, scan, lint or harness. A rule listed here is disabled or refined for this package, with the reason and the date it lapses; a reading model treats the rule as the entry states it.\n\nRULE.TEST.ALLOC_ZERO (scope policy, target *): no in-place operator over element storage exists here: this package's ! functions mutate a registry, a dictionary or a string buffer, and an allocation bound on them measures the Julia runtime, not the package [recorded 2026-09-09, expires 2027-09-09]\nRULE.TEST.PASS_AUDIT (scope policy, target *): the traffic audit counts reads and writes per array element; this package holds no array whose traffic is a correctness property, so a frozen row would record the runtime's behaviour and nothing of the package's [recorded 2026-09-09, expires 2027-09-09]\nRULE.TEST.EXAMPLES_RUN (scope policy, target *): this package ships no Literate examples: its literate surface is empty by design, since a worked example of a review instrument is a round, which is recorded under .gbg/rounds/ rather than executed as a script [recorded 2026-09-09, expires 2027-09-09]\nRULE.DOC.LEADING_IDENTITY (scope policy, target *): the leading identity of a docstring is the physical meaning of the quantity it computes; this package computes no physical quantity, so its docstrings lead with what the operation does, which the rubric's own parameterisation already admits [recorded 2026-09-09, expires 2027-09-09]\nRULE.API.POSITIONAL (scope policy, target *): no program decides whether an argument affects the result: the rule is semantic, so no scan or doctor check can exist for it. Its check in RULES.toml is panel — decided by a reading panel inside an instrument that runs, which under RULE.TEST.RULE_WITH_CHECK is an answer and not the unpaid debt weakness names [recorded 2026-09-09, expires 2027-09-09]","title":"Declared deviations — GBGCodingTools"},{"category":"section","location":"#GBGCodingTools","page":"Home","text":"warning: Being rebuilt\nThis package is being rebuilt to a new design, adopted on 2026-09-17: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md in the repository is what it is being built to, and IMPLEMENTATION.md is the order of the work. The round machinery described in earlier versions of this page is gone, and the pages below describe what is in the tree today, which is less than the site once documented and less than the design asks for.","title":"GBGCodingTools"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-is","page":"Home","text":"GBGCodingTools.jl is a private Julia package, on GitHub, holding the review procedures, scans, test engines and writing rules of four Julia research packages: GBGPeriodicFields, GBGIsotropicTurbulence, GBGStreamingStatistics and GBGCloudPhysics. AI coding sessions do much of the writing, testing and reviewing in those four packages; this package holds the rules those sessions are given and the scans, checks and test engines that verify the rules, so the four packages are held to one standard by the same programs.\n\nEach of the four packages keeps only its own data, under its .gbg/ directory: its configuration, the roles its AI reviewers adopt, its ledger of waived rules, and the records of its past reviews. It names one exact released version of GBGCodingTools, and nothing else passes between the package and this one. This package carries a .gbg/ directory too and is checked by its own tools.","title":"What it is"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-review-procedures","page":"Home","text":"The procedures, called instruments, are slash commands inside Claude Code (the AI coding tool the sessions run in); a person types the command, nothing starts them automatically. They are being rebuilt: the machinery that ran them — the rounds, their fix passes, their verifiers and the gate that froze a build while one was open — was deleted on 2026-09-17, and what replaces it is described in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3 and §4. Nothing a package's docs build or test suite runs reads review state any more.","title":"The review procedures"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-command-line-program-gbg","page":"Home","text":"The program gbg is run from a terminal, in the directory of one of the packages. It is invoked as gbg <noun> <verb>, where the noun names what is acted on and the verb what is done, as in gbg sync status; a bare verb is allowed where the noun is obvious, as in gbg doctor. There are 17 verbs, listed on The verbs, and every one exits with 0 when clean, 1 when it found something, 2 when it could not evaluate, 3 when it crashed. The two a person runs most:\n\ngbg doctor — 41 checks that a package is wired correctly (read-only, except that --deep builds and tests it): the installed version matches the pinned one, no generated file was hand-edited, the docs build and the tests call this package.\ngbg scan docs and gbg scan html — 7 scans over docstrings, pages and the built site: banned LaTeX idioms, undocumented arguments, unlinked sibling packages, prose that grew past its baseline.","title":"The command-line program gbg"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-a-package's-docs-build-and-tests-call","page":"Home","text":"GBGCodingTools.makedocs — what a package's docs/make.jl calls instead of Documenter.makedocs. It runs the quick doctor set — the QUICK selection of GBGCodingTools.doctor, the checks that hold on any machine with no build and no network — and the build-failing scans before building the site, and the built-site scans after.\nGBGCodingTools.TestHarness — the engines a package's test/runtests.jl calls: a bound on what a call allocates (@alloc_below); recorded baselines for an in-place operation's reads and writes and for the public method set, failing the test suite when either moves unannounced; an oracle class on every public value-returning operation — analytic, independent, property, relational or structural, and none resting on relational alone; and a run of the witnesses kept from bug hunts.","title":"What a package's docs build and tests call"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-written-rules","page":"Home","text":"The policy is 95 rules of prose, on The rules, each with an id like RULE.DOC.ARGCOV (every argument of a function appears in its docstring): a documentation rubric, the LaTeX idiom, the citation style, the testing protocol, the rules of the four instruments, and git conventions. gbg sync renders the rules into every package, where the AI sessions read them; the scans and the doctor enforce the ones a program can check.","title":"The written rules"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-a-package-adopts-it","page":"Home","text":"Running gbg init in the package writes its .gbg/config.toml. Running gbg sync then renders the copy of the rules, the four slash commands and a managed block of the package's CLAUDE.md, and writes GBGCodingTools = \"=X.Y.Z\" into the package's docs and test environments. A small launcher script, also named gbg and installed once per machine by gbg install, runs whichever version the package in the current directory pins.","title":"How a package adopts it"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"Install: the launcher, the pinned version, the dev checkout.\nThe verbs: the closed table of gbg <noun> <verb>.\nThe doctor: every check id.\nThe ledger and the config.\nArchitecture: the full design record with every decision and its evidence.","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-module","page":"Home","text":"","title":"The module"},{"category":"module","location":"#GBGCodingTools","page":"Home","text":"GBGCodingTools\n\nThe one shared tooling package of the GBG Julia packages: the review instruments, the docs-build wrapper and its scans, the test harness, the doctor and the policy prose, at one version pinned by every consumer.\n\nA consumer is a package that uses the tool — one of the four sibling GBG packages, or the tool itself. Each consumer pins the tool to one exact version, named in its .gbg/config.toml (tool.version: a released X.Y.Z, or dev for a checkout), which gbg sync renders into the consumer's sub-environment compat entries. A consumer holds data; the tool holds behaviour; the only thing connecting them is that pinned version.\n\nThis package is being rebuilt to the design in, adopted on 2026-09-17, in the order sets out. The round machinery — the stage machine, the fix passes and their grading, the preflight, the seeds and the recall, the closure gate — was deleted on that day and is filed under docs/design-record/machinery-v1/. What is here now is the deterministic half: the scans, the reflection checks, the test harness, the doctor, the generators and the rulebook prose.\n\nThe terms the rest of the package uses as settled words, bound once here:\n\nAn instrument is a review procedure the owner asks a session to run. The new design names four\n\n; none of them runs from this tree today.\n\nA surface is one named file set of [surfaces]: src, rationale, pages, built_html, …\nA hat is one persona of a reading panel, reading the corpus from a single standpoint.\n\nThe module is a map; every part is documented at its home. The command line and its configuration:\n\nmain — the one entry point of gbg <noun> <verb>; the exit contract is 0 clean, 1 substantive finding, 2 cannot evaluate, 3 crash. The verbs live under src/CLI/, one file per noun, each verb a function returning its exit code.\nConfig — Config.read loads and validates a consumer's .gbg/config.toml.\nmakedocs — the mandatory wrapper around Documenter.makedocs (defined by the Documenter extension; declared in DocLints). It reads no review state and nothing can freeze it.\n\nThe upkeep verbs and the rest of src/CLI/:\n\nSync, Init — rendering the managed files into a consumer, and seeding a new consumer's .gbg/.\nLedger — the reader of .gbg/exemptions.toml; Doctor — the read-only health check; Git — the git questions the verbs ask.\nFleet.jl, Bump.jl, Release.jl, Install.jl, Self.jl, Selftest.jl — the fleet report, the pin bump, the release, the install of the gbg command, gbg self verify and the self-test; Schema.jl — the one validator of every artefact against schema/.\n\nThe engines:\n\nTestHarness, Reflection, Witnesses — the Julia-side engines a consumer's tests call.\nscan_docs, scan_html, register_scan! — the deterministic text and built-HTML scan engine over catalog/scans.toml, one function per scan id under src/Scans/.\nTreeHash — git blob and tree hashing on the SHA stdlib, behind gbg self verify.\nHtmlWalker — the Gumbo walker shared by the consolidator and the built-HTML scans.\nconsolidate — the consolidator: the built site as one page-marked manuscript.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#verbs","page":"The verbs","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\ngbg <noun> <verb> is the whole command line; the table is closed, and every verb below is rendered from its own docstring so the page cannot drift from the code. The exit contract is one for all: 0 clean, 1 a substantive finding, 2 cannot evaluate (a precondition not met, missing input, torn file), 3 crash.","title":"The verbs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#Entry-point","page":"The verbs","text":"","title":"Entry point"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#Machine-and-self","page":"The verbs","text":"","title":"Machine and self"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#Consumer-setup","page":"The verbs","text":"","title":"Consumer setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#Checks-and-scans","page":"The verbs","text":"The round verbs and the two design checks stood here until 2026-09-17. The machinery that ran them is filed under docs/design-record/machinery-v1/, and what replaces it — a run, its stages and its report — is docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §10.","title":"Checks and scans"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/verbs/#Fleet-and-release","page":"The verbs","text":"","title":"Fleet and release"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.main","page":"The verbs","text":"main(args) -> Int\n\nThe one entry point of the command line: the shim execs julia -e 'using GBGCodingTools; exit(GBGCodingTools.main(ARGS))', and every test of a verb calls it directly. Dispatches <noun> <verb> (or a lone noun) through VERBS and returns that function's exit code: 0 clean, 1 substantive finding, 2 cannot evaluate, 3 crash. A --dev first argument sets GBG_DEV=1 so the stamps read dev+<sha>, exactly as the shim's --dev route does. An unknown verb prints the table and returns 2. Output is never truncated.\n\nAn exception escaping a verb is printed with its backtrace to stderr and returns 3, so a crash is never read as a finding. The exceptions are the consumer's own files failing to read or validate — a Config.ConfigError or a Ledger.LedgerError — the documented exit 2, printed without a backtrace. A config the verb has itself just written failing to read back is not that case; it raises Config.ConfigWrittenError and returns 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.main"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.parse_options","page":"The verbs","text":"parse_options(args; valued = String[]) -> Options\n\nParses args into an Options record. Any --name=value is a value whatever valued says; an option named in valued also consumes the following bare argument as its value; every other bare --name is a flag; a lone -- ends option parsing. The arguments are:\n\nargs — the command-line arguments to parse, in order.\nvalued — the names, without the dashes, of the options that take a value.\n\nReturns the parsed options; an option that is in valued but has no value is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.parse_options"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Options","page":"The verbs","text":"Options\n\nThe parsed command line of one verb: the positional arguments in order and the options by name.\n\nThe fields are:\n\npositional — every argument that is not an option, in order.\nflags — the options given without a value (--deep, --dry-run), as a set of names without the dashes.\nvalues — the options given with a value (--only LOAD, --reason \"…\", --profile=core), name to value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Options"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_install","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_install(args) -> Int\n\ngbg install [--dev-checkout <path>]: copies the running copy's bin/gbg to ~/.local/bin/gbg with mode 755 and, with the option, writes the one-line pointer ~/.gbg/dev. Runs from whichever copy Pkg resolved for the calling environment — no depot glob is ever expanded. Returns 0 when the shim is written, and the ~/.gbg/dev pointer with it under --dev-checkout, 2 when the Julia in use is below the floor, when --dev-checkout names a directory that is not a GBGCodingTools checkout, or when ~/.local/bin is not on PATH (the shim would be installed but never found).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_install"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.self_verify","page":"The verbs","text":"self_verify(root = Config.find_root(); io = stdout) -> Int\n\nRecomputes the git tree hash of the copy this process runs from (pkgdir(GBGCodingTools), through TreeHash.tree_hash) and compares it with what Pkg.dependencies() reports for the tool in the active environment and with the git-tree-sha1 every sub-environment manifest under root records; asserts the dependency entry is is_tracking_repo && !is_tracking_path (a Pkg.develop'd copy lives inside the depot, so only the flags tell it apart). The arguments are:\n\nroot — the consumer root whose sub-environment manifests are compared; nothing (no root found) leaves only the active environment's entry to compare against.\nio — where the one line per comparison is printed.\n\nReturns 0 when every comparison agrees, 0 with a WARN when the running copy is a working tree (pinned at dev) or no manifest exists to compare against, and 2 for a disagreement or a developed copy — an edited pinned tree is never allowed to grade a round.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.self_verify"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_self_verify","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_self_verify(args) -> Int\n\ngbg self verify: the command-line face of self_verify; run by every gbg round verb and by gbg gate before anything else. Returns its code: 0, or 2 for an edited or developed pinned copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_self_verify"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_probe","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_probe(args) -> Int\n\ngbg probe [--fail]: exists for the platform verification V2 and nothing else. Prints PROBE OK and returns 0; with --fail prints PROBE FAIL to stderr and returns 2. Reads and writes no file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_probe"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Init.init","page":"The verbs","text":"init(root = pwd(); migrate = false, dry_run = false, io = stdout) -> Int\n\ngbg init [--migrate] [--dry-run]: seeds a consumer. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the package root (the directory holding Project.toml).\nmigrate — also convert the test baselines and a former seed_defects.py, and write .gbg/scratch/commit-message.txt for the migration commit.\ndry_run — print what would be written, write nothing.\nio — where the report goes.\n\nIt writes .gbg/config.toml from the tree, an empty ledger, .gbg/rounds/.keep, a placeholder persona for every domain hat without one (Preflight.resolve_hats binds the term), the seeded-once files that are absent (test/Project.toml, docs/make.jl, test/runtests.jl, test/regressions/, docs/ROADMAP.md, docs/prose_shape.csv), and the tool's entries into docs/, test/ and every sub-environment that lists the tool. It refuses an existing .gbg/config.toml (exit 2) unless dry_run, which previews over one rather than refusing, a preview writing nothing. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init.init"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Init.migrate_baselines","page":"The verbs","text":"migrate_baselines(root = pwd(); dry_run = false, io = stdout) -> Int\n\ngbg migrate baselines: the textual converter from the consumer's existing test files to the three frozen TOML baselines TestHarness reads. The arguments are:\n\nroot — the package root.\ndry_run — print the TOML instead of writing it.\nio — where the report goes.\n\nThe .jl files are read, never included or edited. A literal not found is reported and skipped (exit 0); a literal that does not parse to its closing paren is exit 2 naming the line; an existing TOML is refused (exit 2) unless dry_run. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init.migrate_baselines"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Init.new","page":"The verbs","text":"new(pkgname; parent = pwd(), io = stdout) -> Int\n\ngbg new <Pkg>: init for a fifth package. The arguments are:\n\npkgname — the package name.\nparent — the directory holding the package (or the package root itself).\nio — where the report goes.\n\nThe package tree must already exist with a Project.toml. Besides init, it seeds CLAUDE.md from the skeleton and adds a [[consumer]] row to the dev checkout's fleet.toml. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Init.new"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.sync","page":"The verbs","text":"sync(cfg; check = false, io = stdout, ledger = Ledger.read(cfg)) -> Int\n\ngbg sync [--check]. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\ncheck — compare only, write nothing.\nio — where the status lines go.\nledger — the ledger entries.\n\nIt renders every managed file and block, seeds .claude/WORK_QUEUE.md when absent, writes the tool's entries into every sub-environment Project.toml that lists it, inserts the CI fragments, and rewrites .gbg/managed.json; idempotent. It refuses (exit 2) on a MODIFIED or UNMANAGED file and writes nothing then. With check = true the exit is 1 when anything would change, else 0. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.sync"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Sync.cmd_sync","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_sync(args) -> Int\n\ngbg sync [--check]: the CLI face of Sync.sync over the config found from the working directory. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Sync.cmd_sync"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_config","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_config(args) -> Int\n\ngbg config --toml|--json [--effective]: prints the validated config with every default materialised — the object Config.read() returns in-process. --toml (the default) prints TOML, --json prints JSON; --effective adds the machine facts read at run time (hostname, Threads.nthreads(), the Julia version and the running tool version) under a [effective] table. Returns 0, or 2 when the config cannot be read (the reason is printed to stderr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_config"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_schema","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_schema(args) -> Int\n\ngbg schema <name>: prints schema/<name>.schema.json of the running copy with its defaults, as JSON. Returns 0; an unknown name lists the available schemas to stderr and returns 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_schema"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Doctor.run","page":"The verbs","text":"run(cfg; only = nothing, deep = false, io = stdout, json = false,\n    effective = false) -> (checks, code)\n\nRuns the selected checks over a consumer and prints the table. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nonly — ids, id prefixes or the literal QUICK to restrict the run to; every non-deep check when nothing.\ndeep — include the --deep checks (they run builds, tests and witnesses).\nio — where the report goes.\njson — print the results as JSON instead of the table.\neffective — print the effective config after the table.\n\nA FAIL on an exemptable id that a live scope = \"check\" ledger entry names becomes a WARN carrying the entry's reason. Returns the results and the exit code: 1 on any FAIL, else 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Doctor.run"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Doctor.cmd_doctor","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_doctor(args) -> Int\n\ngbg doctor [--deep] [--json] [--effective] [--only <id-or-prefix>[,…]|QUICK]: exit 2 when the working directory is not a consumer (no valid .gbg/config.toml), else the run's code. Returns it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Doctor.cmd_doctor"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.doctor","page":"The verbs","text":"doctor(cfg, set = :QUICK; io = stderr) -> Int\n\nThe in-process doctor the makedocs wrapper calls. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nset — :QUICK for the quick set, :ALL for every non-deep check.\nio — where the failing lines are printed.\n\nReturns 1 on any FAIL, else 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.doctor"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.cmd_scan","page":"The verbs","text":"cmd_scan(args) -> Int\n\ngbg scan docs [--hard-only] [--only id,…] [--rebaseline prose-shape --reason \"…\"] and gbg scan html <build_dir>: the CLI face of scan_docs and scan_html. Returns the scan exit code; a rebaseline first prints every row it raises, adds or drops (prose_shape_changes), then writes docs/prose_shape.csv and returns 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.cmd_scan"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.scan_docs","page":"The verbs","text":"scan_docs(cfg; hard_only = false, only = nothing, io = stdout) -> (results, code)\n\nRuns every catalogued text scan (and the consumer's [[scans.custom]] rows) over the source surfaces and prints the full report: every hard hit and every triage hit, never truncated. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nhard_only — run the hard tier alone (what makedocs calls).\nonly — a list of ids or id prefixes to restrict the run to.\nio — where the report goes.\n\nThe exit code follows the contract: 2 when any scan is DEAD (measured nothing, or raised), else 1 when any hard scan has a hit, else 0; triage hits never change it. Returns the results and the code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.scan_docs"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.scan_html","page":"The verbs","text":"scan_html(cfg, build_dir; only = nothing, io = stdout) -> (results, code)\n\nRuns the built-HTML scans (runner = \"html\") over the pages under build_dir through the Gumbo walker, applies the ledger and prints the report. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nbuild_dir — the Documenter build directory.\nonly — a list of ids or id prefixes to restrict the run to.\nio — where the report goes.\n\nA missing or empty build is DEAD, exit 2. Returns the results and the exit code as scan_docs does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.scan_html"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.write_public_surface","page":"The verbs","text":"write_public_surface(cfg; pkg = Reflection.load_package(cfg)) -> String\n\nWrites .gbg/scratch/public_surface.json: the package's exported names and the public operations Reflection.package_operations derives (every function with a method in a package module), so the a check can ask whether each has a public home. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\npkg — the loaded package module (loaded from the config when not given).\n\nReturns the path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.write_public_surface"},{"category":"module","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Fleet","page":"The verbs","text":"Fleet\n\nThe fleet scorecard: Fleet.rows computes one row per consumer of fleet.toml — OK, NOT-INITIALISED, BEHIND or RED with a detail. The exit contract is D4's: 0 when every row is OK; 1 on NOT-INITIALISED, RED, or BEHIND outside the 7-day sweep window; 2 when a listed path does not exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Fleet"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Fleet.latest_tag","page":"The verbs","text":"latest_tag(root) -> Union{VersionNumber,Nothing}\n\nThe newest released vX.Y.Z tag of the repository at root (prereleases excluded), or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Fleet.latest_tag"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.Fleet.rows","page":"The verbs","text":"Fleet.rows(; deep = false) -> (rows, code)\n\nOne row per [[consumer]]: (name, pin, status, detail). A consumer without .gbg/config.toml is NOT-INITIALISED; one whose QUICK doctor fails is RED with the failing ids; one whose pinned version is older than the fleet's newest is BEHIND with both versions and the window state; else OK. The tool's own row (path = \".\") shows dev+<sha>[-dirty] with clean at vX.Y.Z or off tag, is OK/RED by its own doctor and never BEHIND. A missing path is exit 2. The arguments are:\n\ndeep — run every check, the --deep ones included, as each consumer's doctor pass instead of the QUICK set.\n\nReturns the rows and the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.Fleet.rows"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.bump","page":"The verbs","text":"bump(cfg, ver; io = stdout) -> Int\n\nMoves the pinned version of the consumer at cfg[\"root\"] to ver (a released X.Y.Z); rewrites the [tool] version line of .gbg/config.toml, runs sync (which re-renders every managed file and the sub-environment entries), writes .gbg/scratch/bump-message.txt from the template, and records the [sweep] window in the dev checkout's fleet.toml when the bump leaves the fleet at two versions. The arguments are:\n\ncfg — the validated config.\nver — the target version.\nio — where the report goes.\n\nReturns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.bump"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.release","page":"The verbs","text":"release(ver; io = stdout, skip_tests = false, root = pkgdir(GBGCodingTools)) -> Int\n\nTags and pushes v<ver> from the dev checkout. The arguments are:\n\nver — the version to tag, X.Y.Z or X.Y.Z-rc.N.\nio — where the checks are reported.\nskip_tests — skips Pkg.test(), the docs build and the deep doctor; for the self-tests of this verb only, never set by the CLI.\nroot — the checkout to release, the running package directory by default; the CLI never passes it. A keyword because the verb read pkgdir(GBGCodingTools) directly and so had no test at all. Every refusal below reads root, the dev-checkout guard included: root must be a git working tree, since it is what gets tagged and pushed.\n\nRefused (exit 2) unless: the tree is clean and on the configured branch; the tag is absent; CHANGELOG.md carries ## <ver> —; Project.toml's version equals the release version (the base version for a prerelease); Pkg.test() is green; the tool's own docs build exits 0 (SELF.DOCS); gbg doctor --deep is green at the root. Nothing else holds a release — no review, no open finding, no unanswered question, no audit. A tag is never moved or re-issued. Returns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.release"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/verbs/#GBGCodingTools.selftest","page":"The verbs","text":"selftest(; pinned = false, io = stdout) -> Int\n\nRuns the running copy's own Pkg.test() from a temporary environment that develops it by path, so the self-test's arms run over the fixture wherever the tool is installed. With pinned, additionally copies the fixture to ${TMPDIR}/gbg-pinned-fixture/, pins it at the newest released tag (its sub-environment sources rewritten to the https url and rev), instantiates its docs/ environment and runs the QUICK doctor there — the pinned-copy behaviours (PIN.SELF, PIN.TREESHA, PIN.SOURCES) — leaving the copy in place for the install phase. The arguments are:\n\npinned — also runs the pinned-copy arm described above; false stops after the Pkg.test() run.\nio — where the report and the child processes' output go.\n\nReturns the exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGCodingTools.selftest"}]
}
